The full reduction to cumulative \(\ell^-\)-mass variables is now accepted in the formal left-limit model. Use this as the base regression test for any two-level surplus/debt recursion.

The next genuinely hard cases are unequal low levels, repeated droughts sharing future highs, and cumulative high debt across many episodes. For unequal lows, keep the reduced drought LP as a candidate lemma, not yet an accepted theorem. The first regression tests are:

- two-low path \(0,0.4,0.9,1\), whose reduced-LP value \(68/225\) exceeds the naive last-low value \(0.18\);
- three-low path \(0,0.2,0^8,0.3,0,0.4,1\), whose reduced-LP value \(2.91\) exceeds the naive last-low value \(2.88\).

Try deriving a pooling/PAVA-like or nested resource-allocation rule from the unequal-low dual only after the full-to-reduced LP proof is written.

