./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:33:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\). Proof: \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\), while \(\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h)\).
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:67:If policies may depend on both \(P\) and \(T\), and no computability/statistical-access restriction is imposed, then for every \(\varepsilon>0\) there is a deterministic constant-quote policy \(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}\) with \(R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le\varepsilon\). Choose an \(\varepsilon\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and play it every round. Thus every exogenous process is learnable in this oracle regime, independent of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta,\Gamma\). This does not apply to \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, endogenous/adaptive prices, horizon-oblivious policies, or one learner required to work over a class.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:79:Equivalently, an epsilon-comparator version gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\) using quotes \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\). This is a lower bound for a learner not knowing the continuation, not an instance-wise lower bound for a process-aware learner.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:397:Assume the learner is path-aware/horizon-oblivious and may choose at time \(t\) an actual quote distribution whose payoff against every \(c\in C_t\) is at least \(c-\delta(C_t)-\varepsilon_t\), where \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\). Then for every horizon,
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:399:R_T\le \sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)+\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:427:then posting \(b-\varepsilon_t\) with \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\) yields \(o(T)\) regret. This explains why the alternating path is easy despite linear suffix-menu cost. The reduction fails for endogenous/reactive prices and is not a causal online algorithm.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:455:In the original strict-crossing action space this is an infimum statement, implemented with quotes just below finitely many thresholds. If \(D_n(P)\ge\epsilon n\) infinitely often, every horizon-oblivious expected-regret policy on that path has \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge\epsilon\). The dual recovers singleton terminal blocks as easy, the \(0^N,1/2,1\) block as the basic \(1/4\) conflict, and equal-revenue blocks as the accepted \(1/e\)-type obstruction after the existing block accounting.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:578:**CL-044: Two-level single-drought damping identity.**  
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:595:The primal quotes \(h^-\) off the \(G\) drought coordinates and places total \(\ell^-\)-mass \(S/h\) on those coordinates; the low-horizon and final-high deficits both become \(S(1-\ell/h)\). The matching dual puts weights \(1-\ell/h\) and \(\ell/h\) on \(T_0,T_1\) and uses
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:599:in the formal threshold compactification. Strict crossing in the original action space requires \(\eta\)-implementation. The identity is local and does not imply additive decomposition across many droughts.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:613:**CL-047: Post-low single-drought value is independent of the post-low gap.**  
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:633:and quote times after the low have no positive future before the final high. The primal quotes \(h^-\) by default and replaces total mass \(S/h\) by \(\ell^-\) on the \(G\) conflict coordinates. This makes the low/intermediate-horizon deficit and final-high deficit both equal \(S(1-\ell/h)\). The dual uses weights \(1-\ell/h\) on the low horizon \(r\) and \(\ell/h\) on the final high \(n\); old high and post-low final-only coordinates cancel, while each conflict coordinate contributes the pointwise support cost \(\ell\). The result is an infimum statement under strict crossing, implemented by \(\ell-\eta_t,h-\eta_t\). It is local and does not imply additivity across multiple droughts.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:635:**CL-048: Equal-low multi-low single-drought last-low formula.**  
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:661:Old high protected coordinates are matched by \(h^-\); post-last-low coordinates are final-high-only and are optimally \(h^-\); intermediate zero horizons duplicate the latest low-horizon constraint. In the original strict-crossing action space this is an infimum statement implemented with \(\ell-\eta_t,h-\eta_t\). The last-low rule must not be generalized to unequal low levels.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:699:Nevertheless the horizon-oblivious path-aware policy posting \(1-\varepsilon_t\), with average \(\frac1T\sum_{t\le T}\varepsilon_t\to0\), has \(o(T)\) regret for all horizons: it nearly matches the comparator \(1^-\), losing only the accumulated \(\varepsilon_t\)'s plus \(O(1)\) near horizons ending at a \(1/2\). Hence CL-033 is only a sufficient condition; the local bound can be loose because it compares the fixed comparator to \(\sum_tM_{t,T}\), not to the exact \(\sup_a\sum_t a1\{a<M_{t,T}\}\).
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:775:Iteration 15 adds a sharper two-level local regression test, CL-044, and refutes the proposed \(Q_T\)-based dichotomy via CL-046. The revised positive target is a resolved-surplus or debt recursion: low-horizon surplus \(S\) should matter only relative to the prefix length at the high that resolves it, and low-level quote mass creates persistent high debt that must be controlled cumulatively across droughts. This remains unproved, especially with multiple lows in one drought or overlapping drought intervals.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:777:Iteration 16 promotes the post-low single-drought extension CL-047: post-low zeros do not add local conflict beyond the resolving high debt. This strengthens the evidence that simple dilution is exact for one drought. The remaining gluing target shifts to multiple lows, unequal lows, and repeated droughts, where front-loading constraints and shared high debt may interact.
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:779:Iteration 17 promotes the equal-low multi-low drought formula CL-048. The next candidate is the unequal-low single-drought reduced LP. For
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:783:with low set \(\mathcal L=\{\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_r\}\), useful segment capacities \(B_0=N_0+\mathbf 1_{\{M\ge1\}}\), \(B_i=N_i+1\) for \(1\le i\le r-1\), and segment/low maxima \(m_{i,j}=\max_{q=i+1,\ldots,j}\ell_q\), reports propose the reduced LP
./working_notes/claim_ledger.md:796:This LP is not yet accepted as the full \(D_n\) reduction. It is, however, a useful candidate and already refutes any naive unequal-low "last low only" closed form inside the reduced model: the two-low path \(0,0.4,0.9,1\) has reduced-LP value \(68/225\), larger than the last-low value \(0.18\).
./iterations/iter_015/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Mohsen Heidari, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Useful terminology for separating fixed-design/path-known regret from sequential or universal regret, matching the run's quantifier issues.","title":"Sequential vs. Fixed Design Regrets in Online Learning","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ISIT50566.2022.9834776","venue_year":"IEEE ISIT 2022"},{"authors":"Angelos Assos, Idan Attias, Yuval Dagan, Constantinos Daskalakis, Maxwell K. Fishelson","relevance":"Potential background for recasting the fixed-envelope criterion as an infinite zero-sum game or best-response-oracle feasibility problem.","title":"Online Learning and Solving Infinite Games with an ERM Oracle","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/assos23a.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Changlong Wu, Ananth Grama, Wojciech Szpankowski","relevance":"Process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are characterized by switching or drought-complexity parameters.","title":"Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v195/","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Vasilis Kontonis, Mingchen Ma, Christos Tzamos","relevance":"Fixed-design online learning with order effects is adjacent to the path-known, horizon-oblivious regime where one sequence must serve many prefixes.","title":"The Gain from Ordering in Online Learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/c9b1fe9c41f1eeec3a659154d575a282-Abstract-Conference.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2023"},{"authors":"Alexander Rakhlin, Karthik Sridharan","relevance":"Predictable-sequence methods are relevant background for paths where delayed fills are predictable or stable-high/stable-low policies nearly match the comparator.","title":"Online Learning with Predictable Sequences","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v30/Rakhlin13.html","venue_year":"COLT/PMLR 2013"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The claimed formula may fail in edge cases such as M=1, S=0, h<2 ell, or because the proposed primal mixing ignores an active horizon. Strict crossing also needs eta-loss bookkeeping.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","counterexample search","finite LP regression"],"summary":"Audit the solver's single-drought damping lemma for the two-level formal left-limit path h^M, 0^N, ell, h. Write a complete primal marginal construction and matching dual lambda certificate for D_{M+N+2}=S(1-ell/h), where S=[ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+]_+. Check all boundary cases and then add the strict-crossing implementation.","title":"Audit Single-Drought Damping","why_promising":"This is the newest concrete claimed progress. If correct, it gives an exact local mechanism explaining how old high prices damp later low/high conflicts; if false, finding the failure will immediately refine the two-level program."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Different droughts may interact through shared early quote marginals, and local S_i certificates may neither add nor localize. A path may need nonstable switching policies not captured by stable high or stable low quotes.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dichotomy theorem","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try to decompose arbitrary two-level paths into high-free drought intervals between h occurrences. Define a surplus or queue process related to Q_T=[ell(L_ell(T)-1)-h(L_h(T)-1)]_+. Prove a dichotomy: either Q_T=o(T) and the stable h quote gives o(T) regret, or some comparable-scale prefix has D_n=Omega(n). Treat the case where h eventually stops separately via a stable ell quote.","title":"Two-Level Drought Decomposition","why_promising":"The solver's lemma and stable-high corollary point to a concrete potential function. A successful dichotomy would settle the two-level finite-alphabet localization problem, the current main sandbox."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the fixed-envelope criterion. The hard part is converting repeated augmented certificates into ordinary D_n mass or a real global obstruction.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","LP duality","localization"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds or horizon weights beyond a prefix. It should reproduce the boundary defect in the separated-block example and the S term in h^M,0^N,ell,h. Then test whether repeated augmented defects force large ordinary D_n at comparable scales, or instead yield a separator from fixed-envelope feasibility.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Prefix LP","why_promising":"Iteration 14 refuted direct charging of boundary defects to shorter-prefix D_m because late shadow objectives are invisible. Keeping those shadows explicitly is the natural next localization object."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Simple separated blocks are known to be diluted, and fresh quote opportunities may make every proposed separator learnable. Numerical evidence alone would not prove a separator.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite LP experiment","construction"],"summary":"Search for a genuine market path with D_n=o(n) but no fixed sublinear envelope by chaining many damped two-level droughts. Tune M_i,N_i so each local conflict is damped relative to total prefix length, while the horizon-optimal quote keeps moving enough to defeat any fixed marginal sequence. Verify candidates against the exact finite-prefix LP, not only stable-quote regret.","title":"Damped-Drought Separator Search","why_promising":"If the gluing implication is false, the solver's damping lemma suggests the separator must exploit many individually small but projectively incompatible shadow conflicts rather than one large block."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The two-level positive-part simplification may not extend cleanly. Nested maxima over comparator levels and learner-side response levels can destroy uncrossing or total-unimodularity arguments.","suggested_lenses":["finite alphabet","proof strategy","recursive potential"],"summary":"Generalize two-level damping to finite alphabets c_1<...<c_k. Use the finite-alphabet dual with next-hit times N_j(t) and last-exceedance times L_j(T) to look for a recursive record-level surplus queue: higher stable comparators damp lower-level conflicts unless a lower-level surplus becomes visible at comparable scale.","title":"Recursive Finite-Alphabet Potential","why_promising":"The two-level case may be too special. A k-level potential would turn the finite-alphabet reduction into structural progress and could expose exactly where nested maxima block localization."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Numerical artifacts can mislead, especially with strict-crossing left limits and near-degenerate dual optima. Small n may miss asymptotic phenomena.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","dual extraction","counterexample search"],"summary":"Build an exact finite-prefix LP experiment harness for finite alphabets in the formal left-limit model. For two-level paths, compare D_n to the stable-high gap Q_T, single-drought S values, and sums of local drought surpluses. Extract dual supports for repeated droughts, alternating paths with h<2 ell, and random interleavings.","title":"Finite-Prefix LP Harness","why_promising":"The current frontier is pattern discovery inside a concrete finite LP. A reliable experiment suite would quickly falsify overstrong decomposition claims and produce dual certificates for explorers to prove."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"External infinite-game and fixed-design results may only repackage compactness or require assumptions absent from market suffix maxima. They should be treated as formulation tools, not imported theorems.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","formal reduction","algorithmic formulation"],"summary":"Recast the fixed-envelope condition as a countably constrained zero-sum game: the learner chooses marginal laws, the adversary chooses horizons or horizon mixtures, and best responses are fixed quotes or finite-prefix dual certificates. Compare this to infinite-game ERM-oracle, fixed-design online learning, and predictable-sequence frameworks to identify assumptions that would imply gluing.","title":"Infinite-Game Fixed-Design Formulation","why_promising":"The web search surfaced adjacent fixed-design and infinite-game sources not yet in the bibliography. This may yield cleaner terminology and possibly algorithmic sufficient conditions between stable-comparator cases and the full compactness criterion."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:33:Failed completely for the problem's pseudo-regret. If a policy may depend on both the exogenous law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), it can choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)=\mathbb E_P\sum_t r_t(a)\) and play it every round. This gives \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process, so no nontrivial tail condition can be necessary under this quantifier order. This failure relies on pseudo-regret, exogeneity, and horizon/law awareness.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:37:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:101:but \(\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)=o(T)\) is not necessary even for deterministic known exogenous paths. The alternating path \(1/2,1,1/2,1,\ldots\) has linear \(\sum_t\delta(C_t)\), while quoting \(1-\varepsilon_t\) with vanishing average \(\varepsilon_t\) gives \(o(T)\) regret. The local proof upper-bounds the comparator by \(\sum_tM_{t,T}\), which can be much larger than the value of one fixed comparator quote.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:180:Surviving route: track resolved low-surplus relative to the later high that resolves it, and account for persistent high debt created by low-level quote mass. Multi-low droughts and interactions across droughts remain open.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:192:This strengthens the warning from FA-030: localization cannot be based on stable-high regret at the low horizon or on the length of the post-low waiting time alone. The next plausible obstruction must involve multiple lows, unequal low levels, overlapping droughts, or cumulative high debt across repeated droughts.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:196:Failed as a reduced-LP heuristic in iteration 17. The accepted equal-low multi-low drought formula depends on all low prices being the same \(\ell\); then cumulative low surplus \(S_j=[\ell(s_j-1)-h(M-1)_+]_+\) is monotone and front-loading \(S_j/h\) low mass dominates earlier low constraints.
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:198:For unequal lows this monotonicity breaks. In the proposed reduced drought LP, even the small path
./working_notes/failed_attempts.md:202:with \(h=1\) has reduced-LP value \(68/225\approx0.3022\), whereas the naive last-low-only expression gives \(1.8(1-0.9)=0.18\). The earlier \(0.4\)-horizon genuinely binds. This is not yet promoted as a full \(D_n\) theorem until the unequal-low full-to-reduced LP proof is written, but it is a decisive regression test against any simple last-low formula for variable low levels.
