./iterations/iter_015/idea_01/idea.md:6:Audit the solver's single-drought damping lemma for the two-level formal left-limit path h^M, 0^N, ell, h. Write a complete primal marginal construction and matching dual lambda certificate for D_{M+N+2}=S(1-ell/h), where S=[ell(M+N)-h(M-1)_+]_+. Check all boundary cases and then add the strict-crossing implementation.
./working_notes/counterexamples.md:16:Notes: This uses \(0\in[0,1]\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), the specific witness disappears once all spike heights are below \(\epsilon\). If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions remain, a related supremum pathology remains but needs a separate admissible learner argument.
./working_notes/counterexamples.md:62:Choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and post it in every round. The learner's expected reward equals \(V_T^P(a_T)\), so its pseudo-regret is at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta_T,\Gamma_T\), or any other tail statistic. This is not an algorithmic result unless the process law and horizon are known and approximate optimization is allowed.
./working_notes/counterexamples.md:139:Zero atoms are also only a scaling device. If \(\Pr(Z=0)=1-q\) and \(\Pr(Z=1)=q\), then \(\rho(Z)=q\) and \(\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=0\). Treating the zero atom as consuming one of the \(m\) support points or collapsing \(0\) to \(\rho\) before conditioning on \(Z>0\) changes the problem being bounded.
./working_notes/counterexamples.md:193:But the horizon-oblivious known-path policy \(a_t=1-\varepsilon_t\), with \(\frac1T\sum_{t\le T}\varepsilon_t\to0\), has \(o(T)\) regret. At even horizons it nearly matches the comparator \(1^-\) on all previous quotes. At odd horizons it loses only the same accumulated \(\varepsilon_t\)'s plus an \(O(1)\) boundary effect near the final \(1/2\). Thus the compact suffix-menu bound is sufficient only, not necessary.
