./iterations/iter_005/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:38:- **Sparse vanishing spikes:** explorer’s test passes: thresholds of scale \(\epsilon\) give \(\kappa\le \epsilon\), unlike raw \(\beta\).
./iterations/iter_014/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Jerome Renault","relevance":"Useful analogy for the current gluing problem because it characterizes uniform convergence of dynamic-programming values via total boundedness, suggesting a possible stability condition beyond scalar finite-prefix values.","title":"General limit value in Dynamic Programming","url":"https://doi.org/10.3934/jdg.2014.1.471; https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.0451","venue_year":"Journal of Dynamics and Games, 2014"},{"authors":"Christoph Dann, Yishay Mansour, Mehryar Mohri, Jon Schneider, Balasubramanian Sivan","relevance":"Provides l_infinity/pseudonorm approachability tools for many simultaneous regret constraints, potentially relevant to horizon-indexed deficit feasibility.","title":"Pseudonorm Approachability and Applications to Regret Minimization","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v201/dann23a.html; https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.01517","venue_year":"ALT / PMLR 201, 2023"},{"authors":"Juan Kuntz, Philipp Thomas, Guy-Bart Stan, Mauricio Barahona","relevance":"Countably infinite LP approximation over bounded measure spaces may supply language or conditional tools for the fixed-envelope infinite feasibility LP, though it is not market-specific.","title":"Approximations of Countably Infinite Linear Programs over Bounded Measure Spaces","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/19M1268847; https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.03658","venue_year":"SIAM Journal on Optimization, 2021"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The boundary defect may be large for algebraic reasons that do not imply a local market conflict; a false inequality here would likely be exposed by stable-singleton or alternating paths.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dual localization","counterexample search"],"summary":"Attack the solver's exact obstruction: replace the crude cross-scale boundary term m min(||u||_1,||v||_1) by an intrinsic defect term. For f_t(w)=sup_a a sum_T w_T 1{a<M_{t,T}}, study f_t(u)+f_t(v)-f_t(u+v) on t<m and try to prove that a large total boundary defect itself yields a finite-prefix D_m certificate. A target conditional inequality is: dual mass split across scales can only create large positive Phi if either one side already has positive Phi or the boundary prefix has D_m=Omega(m).","title":"Boundary Defect Implies Local Conflict","why_promising":"This directly addresses the gap in iteration 14. If true, recursive dyadic splitting would turn failure of a fixed sublinear envelope into comparable-scale finite-prefix certificates, proving D_n=o(n) suffices."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Finite alphabets may still contain the full obstruction, or quantizing arbitrary paths may destroy the strict-crossing/left-limit structure needed for a general theorem.","suggested_lenses":["finite alphabet reduction","laminar intervals","uncrossing proof"],"summary":"Restrict first to paths with prices in a fixed finite alphabet 0<p_1<...<p_k. Rewrite the dual using last-exceedance functions L_j(T)=max{s<=T:P_s>p_j}; the per-time dual term becomes max_j p_j sum_{T:t<L_j(T)} lambda_T. This is a nested interval/laminar structure. Try to prove an uncrossing theorem: any profitable dual certificate can be converted, up to lower-order loss, into one supported on a horizon window comparable to its affected prefix.","title":"Finite-Alphabet Laminar Dual","why_promising":"The last-exceedance representation is the most market-specific structure currently available. A finite-alphabet theorem would be a meaningful positive result and a plausible stepping stone to approximation."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Repeated repairs may not converge, and cumulative future damage from repairing many prefixes may be linear at some horizons. The scalar D_n=o(n) hypothesis may be too weak to provide enough slack for intermediate horizons.","suggested_lenses":["constructive proof","compactness","primal gluing"],"summary":"Try to build the infinite marginal sequence directly by staged repairs. Given a good finite optimizer for a long prefix n and a shorter protected prefix m<<n, replace the first m marginals by ones that repair horizons <=m. Prefix-splicing says all later deficits change by at most m. Explore whether a schedule m_k<<n_k can make cumulative repair damage o(T), yielding a fixed sublinear envelope from D_n=o(n).","title":"Staged Prefix Repair Construction","why_promising":"This uses the accepted prefix-splicing robustness in the primal rather than fighting the dual. It may prove sufficiency without a full localization theorem."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Finite-n artifacts may look like separators but vanish under extension; also E_n-type fixed-envelope proxies need to be chosen carefully to avoid merely restating D_n.","suggested_lenses":["finite LP experiment","counterexample search","regression tests"],"summary":"Implement finite-prefix LP computations over small price alphabets and search for paths where D_m/m is small for every prefix m but a fixed-envelope proxy stays bounded away from zero. Useful proxies include inf_mu max_{T<=n} d_T/T, dyadic-weighted objectives, and constraints requiring d_T<=epsilon_T T for prescribed slowly vanishing epsilon_T. Search over non-block interleavings, nested separated blocks, and alternating low/high record patterns.","title":"LP Search For A Market Separator","why_promising":"A concrete finite counterexample pattern would focus the negative route; failure to find one across structured searches would give evidence for finite-alphabet gluing and identify invariants."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The finite-prefix value may simply add the local kappa losses over many blocks and become linear, killing D_n=o(n). Conversely, a path-aware policy may adapt block by block and avoid global failure.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","block algebra","exact D_n computation"],"summary":"Generalize the failed separated-block attempt from one diluted conflict to many diluted conflicts. Build paths with many small old conflicts, each o(T) at its later revealing horizon, but whose cumulative unresolved incompatibility might be Omega(T) for any fixed policy. Candidate forms are repeated 0^{N_i},c_0,0^{L_i},c_1 modules with alternating threshold roles or moving-scale equal-revenue menus. For each candidate, compute whether D_n adds local conflicts or remains diluted.","title":"Cumulative Diluted Conflicts","why_promising":"The simple separated block fails because one old conflict is diluted. A cumulative construction is the most plausible remaining negative route resembling the abstract parity obstruction while respecting fresh market coordinates."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Approachability may only rederive the fixed-envelope compactness criterion, not the weaker scalar D_n condition. The game is nonstationary because horizon offsets V_T^* and active coordinates change with time.","suggested_lenses":["approachability formulation","infinite-dimensional feasibility","algorithmic interpretation"],"summary":"Formulate the known-path problem as simultaneous approachability of the negative orthant for horizon-indexed deficits. A quote distribution at time t contributes a vector to all coordinates T>t, while V_T^* supplies a fixed target offset. Use l_infinity or pseudonorm approachability duality to ask whether finite supported horizon-dual tests are enough for a uniform strategy, and compare the resulting condition to D_n and the fixed-envelope criterion.","title":"Simultaneous-Horizon Approachability","why_promising":"This may import mature machinery for one sequence serving many criteria and could clarify exactly which uniform condition is missing from D_n=o(n)."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Generic infinite-LP or uniform-value criteria may be too abstract and fail to exploit market suffix-max structure; the output may be only a conditional theorem.","suggested_lenses":["literature transfer","infinite LP","stability condition"],"summary":"Look for a total-boundedness or tightness condition under which finite-prefix feasibility upgrades to fixed-envelope feasibility. Translate Renault-style total boundedness of value families or countably-infinite LP approximation conditions into this setting, perhaps as compactness of normalized last-exceedance profiles or moment bounds on bad dual horizon weights. Then test whether finite alphabets, record-stable paths, or bounded active-threshold paths satisfy it automatically.","title":"Total-Boundedness Gluing Criterion","why_promising":"Even if D_n=o(n) alone remains open, a clean intermediate condition could be the right structural theorem and would explain why generic compactness is insufficient."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"The calculations may become case-heavy and only confirm dilution rather than producing a theorem. For m>2, strict left-limit and intermediate-horizon corrections need careful handling.","suggested_lenses":["exact calculation","stress test","proof audit"],"summary":"Finish and generalize the separated-block calculation. Compute D_n exactly, or within o(n), for paths of the form 0^{N_0},c_0,0^{N_1},c_1,...,0^{N_k},c_k with increasing thresholds and varying gaps. Determine whether the finite-prefix value is a max of active local conflicts, a sum, or a more subtle convolution. Use these formulas as regression tests for both localization and separator attempts.","title":"Separated Multi-Block Formula","why_promising":"Iteration 14's exact two-threshold separated block explains one failed counterexample. A general formula would sharply delimit which block constructions can or cannot separate D_n=o(n) from fixed-envelope feasibility."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:9:The right proof strategy should refine the dichotomy by including the time at which a low-surplus drought is resolved by the next high. Linear obstruction seems to require surplus linear relative to the resolving high prefix, not merely linear relative to the low horizon.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:20:A stronger local drought statement appears valid. For
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:47:**Resolved-Drought Lemma Candidate.** In a drought whose last high is at \(H\), whose last low before the next high is at \(R\), and whose next high is at \(G\), the relevant absolute low surplus is
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:57:**Prefix Without Resolving High.** A prefix ending inside the drought before the next high need not have positive \(D_n\). The learner can quote \(h^-\) before the last high and \(\ell^-\) after it, matching or exceeding the fixed comparator on the low-only prefix.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:61:The natural proof route is to write a drought LP. Let \(Y_r\) be cumulative probability mass assigned to \(\ell^-\) in the current high-free interval before low horizon \(r\). Then low horizons impose constraints of the form
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:69:For one isolated drought, balancing these gives \(S(1-\ell/h)\).
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:71:The problem is global composition. Low-quote mass chosen in earlier droughts creates persistent high-quote debt at later high horizons. If high-free intervals include long post-low zero tails, this debt can be diluted by time before the next high. Thus local \(S_i\) certificates do not simply add.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:83:Uniform mixing over an entire drought may fail early low horizons; the construction needs cumulative coverage before each low time.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:85:Multiple droughts interact through accumulated high debt. This is the main missing bookkeeping.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:110:- D. V. Lindley. “The theory of queues with a single server.” *Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society* 48(2):277-289, 1952. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100027638. Relevance: background for Lindley-recursion/reflected-queue analogies that may help formalize the drought surplus/debt process; not a direct market-making theorem.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:114:1. Audit and polish the single-drought lemma, including the \(0^L\) post-low extension.
./iterations/iter_015/idea_02/explorer_01.md:116:3. Build the explicit switching-policy upper bound for diluted droughts.
./iterations/iter_005/idea_02/explorer_01.md:130:Sparse vanishing spikes: if all relevant thresholds in a long block are at most \(\epsilon\), then choosing \(\mu=\delta_0\) gives \(\kappa\le\epsilon\). Thus the modulus vanishes with payoff scale, unlike raw \(\beta\).
./logs/iter_001_ideas.stderr.log:1020:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari","relevance":"Primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction for online learning; supports the solver's finite-grid truncated expert upper bound.","title":"Online Learning under Delayed Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2013"},{"authors":"Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi","relevance":"Gives full-information adversarial-delay regret in terms of cumulative delay, relevant to replacing a fixed truncation horizon by delay-adaptive bounds.","title":"Online Learning with Adversarial Delays","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2015"},{"authors":"Tobias Sommer Thune, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Introduces unrestricted-delay bandit techniques and skipping of excessively delayed feedback, a likely template for sharper market-making conditions.","title":"Nonstochastic Multiarmed Bandits with Unrestricted Delays","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/8881-nonstochastic-multiarmed-bandits-with-unrestricted-delays","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2019"},{"authors":"Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined skip-set guarantees; useful terminology for excessive-delay robustness.","title":"An Optimal Algorithm for Adversarial Bandits with Arbitrary Delays","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.06054","venue_year":"AISTATS 2020"},{"authors":"Andras Gyorgy, Pooria Joulani","relevance":"Delay- and data-adaptive adversarial bandit bounds, including skipping; may inspire bounds depending on delayed payoff mass rather than unweighted delay probability.","title":"Adapting to Delays and Data in Adversarial Multi-Armed Bandits","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.06022","venue_year":"ICML 2021"},{"authors":"Tal Lancewicki, Shahar Segal, Tomer Koren, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Uses delay quantiles and permits infinite delays in stochastic bandits; relevant to replacing uniform tail assumptions by quantile/truncation penalties.","title":"Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits with Unrestricted Delay Distributions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/lancewicki21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Genevieve E. Flaspohler, Francesco Orabona, Judah Cohen, Soukayna Mouatadid, Miruna Oprescu, Paulo Orenstein, Lester Mackey","relevance":"Develops optimistic delayed online-learning algorithms and reductions, potentially useful for tuning-free full-information expert implementations.","title":"Online Learning with Optimism and Delay","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/flaspohler21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Paul Duetting, Guru Guruganesh, Jon Schneider, Joshua Ruizhi Wang","relevance":"One-sided Lipschitz maximization is structurally close to threshold/posting-price reward curves and may help improve discretization or exploit monotonicity.","title":"Optimal No-Regret Learning for One-Sided Lipschitz Functions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/duetting23b.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Lukas Zierahn, Dirk van der Hoeven, Tal Lancewicki, Aviv Rosenberg, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi","relevance":"Recent unified delayed-feedback analysis for nonstochastic bandit settings; useful for modern delay decomposition and total-delay terminology.","title":"A Unified Analysis of Nonstochastic Delayed Feedback for Combinatorial Semi-Bandits, Linear Bandits, and MDPs","url":"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v26/24-0496.html","venue_year":"JMLR 2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"If the intended theorem is minimax over a class of processes rather than literal per-process learnability, the sparse-spike counterexample may only expose a quantifier mismatch.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","quantifier analysis","counterexample generalization"],"summary":"Verify the solver's sparse vanishing-spike refutation line by line: beta(h)=1 via action a=0 at spike times, the best fixed comparator has expected payoff at most 1 for every horizon, and the continuum maximum exists. Then try to strengthen it to a family with beta=1 and regret bounded by any prescribed sublinear envelope.","title":"Audit the Refutation","why_promising":"This is the shortest path to a rigorous conclusion about the stated iff: the unweighted tail condition appears to count long delays at actions with negligible payoff."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The essential supremum over histories may still be too strong; rare histories or isolated times can violate gamma without causing linear expected regret.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","condition refinement","counterexample search"],"summary":"Replace beta by the payoff-weighted tail gamma(h)=sup_t sup_a esssup a Pr(h<tau_t(a)<infty | history). Prove the same upper bound with T gamma(H) in place of T beta(H), and show equivalence to uniform tightness over positive-scale actions a>=epsilon for every epsilon>0. Then test whether gamma(h)->0 is necessary or only sufficient.","title":"Payoff-Weighted Tail","why_promising":"It directly repairs the solver's obstruction: long delays at a=0 or tiny quote levels should not matter for regret."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Necessity may fail because an algorithm can exploit predictable price structure rather than waiting for delayed reward feedback.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","necessity test","rare-history examples"],"summary":"Study the ex-ante horizon-level truncation error Delta_T(H)=sup_a sum_{t<=T} E[a 1{H<tau_t(a)<=T-t}]. Establish sufficiency when some H_T has H_T log T=o(T) and Delta_T(H_T)=o(T), using the same delayed-grid reduction. Investigate whether this is closer to a true characterization than uniform gamma.","title":"Horizon Tail Error","why_promising":"This is the weakest condition visible from the truncation proof and avoids beta's worst-case sup over irrelevant times and histories."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Failure of beta alone is too weak for lower bounds; any theorem will likely need positive payoff scale, positive density in time, and unpredictability assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao construction","indistinguishability"],"summary":"If the intended statement is uniform over a class of processes, formulate a lower-bound condition based on indistinguishable blocks: two laws share the same observed prefix for H rounds but differ in whether a valuable quote crosses after the delay. Use Yao or Le Cam to force regret from actions chosen before the delayed signal arrives.","title":"Minimax Necessity","why_promising":"A defensible only-if theorem must convert meaningful long-tail mass into information-theoretic hardness; this direction isolates the needed quantifiers."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Naively summing delays over all grid arms may be far too pessimistic; the explorer must exploit full-information and nested-threshold structure.","suggested_lenses":["algorithmic reduction","known delayed-learning bounds","proof strategy"],"summary":"Replace fixed-H truncation with arbitrary-delay or skipping delayed-expert algorithms. For grid actions, reveal each reward when its crossing occurs or at horizon, and derive regret in terms of total counted delay, outstanding observations, or weighted skipped payoff. Compare the resulting condition to beta, gamma, and Delta_T.","title":"Skipping Bounds","why_promising":"Modern delayed-learning theory already handles excessive and unbounded delays; translating skip-set bounds may yield a sharper sufficient condition than uniform tail decay."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Existing one-sided Lipschitz or pricing results often assume immediate or stochastic feedback, so adapting them may not prove the delayed theorem directly.","suggested_lenses":["structural analysis","adaptive discretization","literature comparison"],"summary":"Exploit the deterministic identity r_t(a)=a 1{max_{s=t+1..T} P_s > a}; the fixed-action payoff is a times the last crossing time of level a. Investigate adaptive grids, one-sided Lipschitz optimization, and threshold nesting to improve the T/K discretization and identify the real obstruction to no-regret.","title":"Threshold Geometry","why_promising":"The market-making problem is more structured than generic delayed experts: one revealed price resolves an interval of quotes, and comparator payoffs are monotone threshold curves."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The outcome will depend heavily on the exact meaning of 'possible' and on whether the learner may depend on the process law.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite-case calculations","condition stress test"],"summary":"Systematically test proposed necessary conditions against constructed processes: rare high-value delayed spikes, deterministic predictable sparse spikes, vanishing-value dense spikes, and isolated bad histories. For each, compute beta, gamma, Delta_T, best fixed comparator payoff, and regret of simple learners.","title":"Stress-Test Conditions","why_promising":"This can prevent replacing the false unweighted beta condition with another overly strong condition before a lower-bound theorem is attempted."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:294:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:779:The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:990:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:995:\forall\epsilon>0,\ \beta_\epsilon(h)\to0.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1033:For every \(\varepsilon>0\), choose \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) with
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1035:V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\ge V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1037:The deterministic policy that posts \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) every round has pseudo-regret at most \(\varepsilon\). This uses the problem's \(\sup_a\mathbb E-\mathbb E\) regret, law and horizon awareness, and no computability constraint. Status: accepted.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1053:An epsilon version comparing to \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\). Status: accepted for universal/minimax lower-bound use.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1214:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1217:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\). Proof: \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\), while \(\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h)\).
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1251:If policies may depend on both \(P\) and \(T\), and no computability/statistical-access restriction is imposed, then for every \(\varepsilon>0\) there is a deterministic constant-quote policy \(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}\) with \(R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le\varepsilon\). Choose an \(\varepsilon\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and play it every round. Thus every exogenous process is learnable in this oracle regime, independent of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta,\Gamma\). This does not apply to \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, endogenous/adaptive prices, horizon-oblivious policies, or one learner required to work over a class.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1263:Equivalently, an epsilon-comparator version gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\) using quotes \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\). This is a lower bound for a learner not knowing the continuation, not an instance-wise lower bound for a process-aware learner.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1690:Failed completely for the problem's pseudo-regret. If a policy may depend on both the exogenous law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), it can choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)=\mathbb E_P\sum_t r_t(a)\) and play it every round. This gives \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process, so no nontrivial tail condition can be necessary under this quantifier order. This failure relies on pseudo-regret, exogeneity, and horizon/law awareness.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1694:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1752:Notes: This uses \(0\in[0,1]\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), the specific witness disappears once all spike heights are below \(\epsilon\). If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions remain, a related supremum pathology remains but needs a separate admissible learner argument.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1798:Choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and post it in every round. The learner's expected reward equals \(V_T^P(a_T)\), so its pseudo-regret is at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta_T,\Gamma_T\), or any other tail statistic. This is not an algorithmic result unless the process law and horizon are known and approximate optimization is allowed.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1875:Zero atoms are also only a scaling device. If \(\Pr(Z=0)=1-q\) and \(\Pr(Z=1)=q\), then \(\rho(Z)=q\) and \(\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=0\). Treating the zero atom as consuming one of the \(m\) support points or collapsing \(0\) to \(\rho\) before conditioning on \(Z>0\) changes the problem being bounded.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2429:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous policy-independent prices and the problem's pseudo-regret, if a learner may depend on both the law \(P\) and horizon \(T\), it can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)\) and get \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2430:- Accepted the finite two-continuation lower bound: for \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner has regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation, using `sup` comparator or an epsilon-comparator variant.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2461:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous prices and pseudo-regret, a law-and-horizon-aware learner can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote and get \(o(T)\) regret for every process.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2915:./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:2937:./iterations/iter_009/idea_01/idea.md:12:The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.
./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:3454:./iterations/iter_005/idea_02/explorer_01.md:130:Sparse vanishing spikes: if all relevant thresholds in a long block are at most \(\epsilon\), then choosing \(\mu=\delta_0\) gives \(\kappa\le\epsilon\). Thus the modulus vanishes with payoff scale, unlike raw \(\beta\).
