n","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_017_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stderr.log:980:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
