n-15 target and gives the cleanest local regression test for any two-level debt recursion."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Earlier low horizons may not be dominated by the final low; capacity constraints on where low-mass can be placed may make the LP more complex than the solver's sketch suggests.","suggested_lenses":["finite LP duality","cumulative scheduling"],"summary":"Generalize to one high-free episode with several low prices: h^M,0^{N_0},l,0^{N_1},l,...,0^{N_r},l,0^L,h. Formulate the exact LP with cumulative low-horizon surplus constraints S_j - l P_j and final high debt (h-l)P_total. Determine whether the last low alone binds or whether early lows create additional front-loading constraints.","title":"Multi-Low Single-Drought LP","why_promising":"This is the smallest nontrivial test of the proposed resolved-surplus/debt picture."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Small finite examples may mislead, and numerical LP output will still need exact rational or symbolic proof before promotion.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","counterexample search"],"summary":"Implement the formal left-limit finite-prefix LP for two-level paths and enumerate short paths over {0,l,h}, plus parametric repeated diluted droughts. Extract primal marginals and dual supports; compare D_n against the solver formula, the stable-high gap Q_T, and candidate debt recursions.","title":"Exact Two-Level LP Experiments","why_promising":"The gluing problem has repeatedly defeated intuition; exact finite-prefix computations can quickly falsify proposed recursions or reveal stable invariants."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Local drought costs may not add because the same quote marginals serve overlapping future horizons; old high history can damp later conflicts nonlocally.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","queueing analogy"],"summary":"Try to define a two-level resolved-surplus/debt recursion. A low-dominated drought creates surplus relative to old high history; assigning low quote mass reduces low regret but creates high debt at the next resolving high. Seek upper and lower bounds for D_n or fixed-envelope feasibility via a reflected-workload invariant.","title":"Resolved-Surplus Debt Recursion","why_promising":"It directly targets the current bottleneck: replacing the refuted raw Q_T localization by a state variable that remembers both surplus and future high debt."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the global fixed-envelope criterion unless the shadow objectives are restricted in a useful way.","suggested_lenses":["dual localization","boundary certificate"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds/horizon weights when splitting a dual certificate. Use the exact boundary identity to ask whether repeated boundary defects either produce comparable-scale D_n mass or are discharged by measurable future quote opportunities.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Localization","why_promising":"It directly addresses the known failure of charging boundary defect to the shorter real prefix."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Fresh quote opportunities after each low may always allow a path-aware policy to repair old conflicts; cumulative checks may make D_n linear after all.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","gluing stress test"],"summary":"Search for an infinite market-path separator between D_n=o(n) and the fixed-envelope criterion using repeated diluted droughts, with L_i much larger than N_i but N_i arranged so low-horizon demands recur. Calibrate each block using the solver's local formula; if no separator appears, prove gluing for this restricted class.","title":"Repeated Diluted-Drought Separator","why_promising":"Simple separated blocks failed because one old conflict is diluted; repeated diluted conflicts are the next plausible negative construction."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Branch observability and horizon-aware pseudo-regret can trivialize the construction; the transfer lemma requires exact same-prefix laws and probability-weighted accounting.","suggested_lenses":["stochastic counterexample","conditional transfer"],"summary":"Use rare branches carrying long multi-threshold or drought gadgets to formalize an essential-sup obstruction: beta or gamma remains large on rare histories, while expected regret contribution p_i N_i kappa_i is o(T). Compute the exact p_i,N_i,L_i conditions and state which learner quantifiers it refutes.","title":"Rare-Branch Hardness Calibration","why_promising":"It would cleanly separate uniform conditional tail failure from expected decision-relevant regret, strengthening the counterexample bank."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Existing delayed-reward literature is mostly not this suffix-horizon full-information model; discontinuous threshold rewards and exogeneity assumptions must be mapped carefully.","suggested_lenses":["literature translation","algorithmic sufficiency"],"summary":"Explore adaptive truncation/skipping algorithms for the finite-grid reduction using outstanding-observation or priority-queue ideas from delayed online learning. Aim for regret bounds in terms of skipped payoff-weighted unresolved fills rather than a fixed H or raw beta tail.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"This is independent of the known-path gluing problem and may yield a cleaner positive theorem aligned with modern delayed-feedback terminology."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
