./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:3:The assigned idea is sound and important: if “sublinear regret is possible” allows the learner to depend on both the process law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), the learning problem collapses. For every process and every horizon, a learner can simply play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed comparator quote at every round. This gives regret at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta\), \(\gamma\), or any tail condition.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:16:Because rewards are bounded by \(T\), \(V_T^{*,P}<\infty\). For every \(\varepsilon>0\), there exists \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\in[0,1]\) with
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:18:V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\ge V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:20:The deterministic policy that posts \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) at every time has expected reward exactly \(V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\), hence regret at most \(\varepsilon\).
