./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:29:R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le \varepsilon.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:52:Let \(\varepsilon_T=1\), or any \(o(T)\) sequence. For each \(T\), pick \(a_T\) satisfying
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:54:V_T^P(a_T)\ge \sup_{a\in[0,1]}V_T^P(a)-\varepsilon_T.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:62:V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon_T.
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:64:Therefore \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\).
./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01.stdout.log:66:This also handles the existing maximum-attainment issue: no max is needed, only a supremum and an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer.
./iterations/iter_002/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Andras Gyorgy, Pooria Joulani","relevance":"Useful for adaptive delay/skipping bounds that may replace a fixed truncation horizon in the grid market-making reduction.","title":"Adapting to Delays and Data in Adversarial Multi-Armed Bandits","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/gyorgy21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 139, 2021"},{"authors":"Genevieve E. Flaspohler, Francesco Orabona, Judah Cohen, Soukayna Mouatadid, Miruna Oprescu, Paulo Orenstein, Lester Mackey","relevance":"Gives a delay-as-optimism perspective that may help exploit predictable delayed fills rather than charging all long delays as tail loss.","title":"Online Learning with Optimism and Delay","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/flaspohler21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 139, 2021"},{"authors":"Paul Duetting, Guru Guruganesh, Jon Schneider, Joshua Ruizhi Wang","relevance":"Potential tool for exploiting the one-sided threshold geometry of fixed-quote payoff curves and improving naive grid discretization.","title":"Optimal No-Regret Learning for One-Sided Lipschitz Functions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/duetting23b.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 202, 2023"},{"authors":"Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Claudio Gentile, Yishay Mansour, Alberto Minora","relevance":"Contains delayed-bandit minimax background and lower-bound methodology relevant to the two-continuation block lower bound.","title":"Delay and Cooperation in Nonstochastic Bandits","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/cesa-bianchi16.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 49, 2016"},{"authors":"Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari","relevance":"Full-information delayed OCO black-box reductions may support a cleaner proof of the finite-grid sufficiency theorem and horizon-free variants.","title":"Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms","url":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/10320","venue_year":"AAAI, 2016"},{"authors":"Robert D. Kleinberg, Frank Thomson Leighton","relevance":"Foundational posted-price regret lower bounds; relevant to testing whether the quote-payoff threshold geometry admits sharper discretization or lower bounds.","title":"The Value of Knowing a Demand Curve: Bounds on Regret for Online Posted-Price Auctions","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/946243.946352","venue_year":"FOCS, 2003"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"This may mainly clarify definitions rather than produce a new theorem; horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability may still collapse under a different oracle argument.","suggested_lenses":["definition audit","proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Formalize the solver's quantifier-collapse observation: if the learner may depend on both the process law and the horizon, every process is learnable by repeatedly playing a horizon-optimal fixed quote up to an epsilon. Then separate the nontrivial regimes: horizon-oblivious process-aware learners, universal learners over a class, minimax learners, and post-history uniform guarantees.","title":"Pin Down The Nontrivial Quantifiers","why_promising":"It may completely settle which readings of the original iff are impossible before explorers spend effort proving necessity statements that are vacuous or false."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The lower bound may only prove a universal-class or minimax impossibility, not an instance-wise characterization; strict crossing and unattained suprema need careful epsilon handling.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao/minimax","strict-threshold audit"],"summary":"Turn the two-continuation block example into a theorem. Use N initial zeros followed by a terminal price in {1/2, 1}; before the terminal observation the learner cannot know whether quotes below 1/2 or near 1 are needed. Prove that for any randomized learner one continuation has Omega(N) regret, then concatenate superexponentially growing blocks to get a class-level no-sublinear-regret result.","title":"Two-Continuation Indistinguishability Lower Bound","why_promising":"This is the most concrete route from raw tail failure to a real hardness theorem, and it directly targets the information-theoretic obstruction identified in the notes."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A too-exact functional could become tautological or unobservable; proving both sufficiency and necessity may require strong class assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["information theory","functional design","proof strategy"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail quantity that counts unresolved payoff only when it can change the identity or value of the best fixed quote conditional on the current history. Possible forms include a conditional value-of-information gap, a Le Cam two-continuation modulus, or a supremum over pairs of continuations that are observationally identical for h steps but have separated optimal quotes.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Mass","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that raw beta, gamma, and Delta fail because delayed mass is tiny, predictable, or irrelevant. A value-of-information tail is aligned with the actual obstruction."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction must keep expected comparator value small while maintaining positive-payoff essential-sup tails; if the rare branch is fully coded, learnability becomes trivial and the example may overlap CE-003.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon calculations","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Build a rare-branch process with positive-probability histories containing arbitrarily long profitable delays, so beta or gamma fails by essential supremum, while the ex-ante contribution to regret is summable or o(T). A candidate is to draw a rare branch m with probability p_m, reveal or partially reveal a code, wait L_m steps, and then emit a constant-scale random terminal price with p_m L_m summable.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Stress Test","why_promising":"It would show that uniform post-history tail conditions are too strong even at positive payoff scale, and would motivate expectation, quantile, or predictable-tail alternatives."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"These quantities are horizon-dependent and may still be far from necessary for learnability; they may mostly improve sufficiency rather than characterize possibility.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","comparison inequalities","counterexample calculations"],"summary":"Develop the hierarchy between Delta_T(H), the smaller comparator gap Gamma_T(H)=sup_a V_T(a)-sup_a V_T^H(a), and possible argmax-stability gaps. Prove the clean regret comparison R_T <= R_T^H + Gamma_T(H), compute Gamma on CE-001/CE-003/CE-005, and identify when Gamma_T(H_T)=o(T) is enough for truncation-based algorithms.","title":"Sharper Truncation Gap Hierarchy","why_promising":"It gives an immediate sharper sufficient condition and may reveal exactly which part of delayed payoff mass matters to fixed-comparator regret."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit or OCO results, while the market-making reduction is full-information with arm-dependent and infinite non-fill delays; the mapping may require a new wrapper rather than direct citation.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature mapping","model reduction"],"summary":"Translate adaptive-delay and skipping algorithms to the finite grid market-making model. Treat unresolved fills after a chosen cutoff as skipped observations and aim for regret bounded by learning terms plus skipped payoff mass, without knowing beta, gamma, or a good H in advance. Compare residue-class Hedge, Joulani-Gyorgy-Szepesvari reductions, Gyorgy-Joulani skipping, and optimism-with-delay methods.","title":"Adaptive Skipping For Grid Market Making","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a more usable algorithm and connect predictable long delays to optimism rather than worst-case truncation loss."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Adversarial time variation and delayed observation may destroy the strong one-sided-Lipschitz guarantees; naive T/K discretization may be minimax-tight for the delayed adversarial problem.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","adaptive discretization","lower bound"],"summary":"Exploit that each truncated reward is a threshold function r_t^H(a)=a 1{a < M_t^H}. Study whether cumulative payoff curves have one-sided Lipschitz or posted-price structure that permits adaptive grids, binary-search-style localization, or smaller discretization regret than T/K. Cross-check against Kleinberg-Leighton and Duetting-Guruganesh-Schneider-Wang style lower bounds.","title":"Threshold Geometry Beyond Uniform Grids","why_promising":"The current proof treats quotes as unrelated arms after discretization, losing structure that may be crucial for sharp rates or for a cleaner characterization."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This is mostly negative progress unless paired with a positive characterization; all-horizon bounds can be delicate near block boundaries.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample formalization","all-horizon proof","critic audit"],"summary":"Polish the main counterexamples into standalone propositions: sparse vanishing spikes refuting beta-necessity, coded delays refuting gamma-necessity, and predictable spikes refuting Delta-necessity. For CE-005, finish the all-horizons proof for the superexponential uniform-spike variant and decide whether the binary power-of-two variant requires horizon-aware actions.","title":"Counterexample Package With Exact Quantifiers","why_promising":"A rigorous negative package will prevent future agents from re-proving false necessity claims and will clarify exactly which assumptions any revised theorem must include."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:2:Promoted the post-low single-drought identity:
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:11:Kept the equal-low multi-low drought formula as candidate only. It has a clean reduced LP and dual certificate, but still needs the full reduction from \(D_n\) to \(\ell^-/h^-\) marginals and a proof that earlier low horizons are dominated.
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:22:No external theorem was found for the new drought identities.
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:31:1. Prove the equal-low multi-low drought formula rigorously.
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:32:2. Test unequal-low droughts, where early lows may genuinely bind.
./iterations/iter_016/consolidation.md:33:3. Run exact finite-prefix LP experiments for repeated diluted droughts and compare against surplus/debt recursions.
./iterations/iter_016/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Amelie Heliou, Panayotis Mertikopoulos, Zhengyuan Zhou","relevance":"Continuous-action delayed-reward online learning with unbounded/asynchronous feedback; adjacent for adaptive truncation or priority-queue algorithms, but not a direct theorem for suffix-horizon market fills.","title":"Gradient-free Online Learning in Games with Delayed Rewards","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.10911","venue_year":"ICML 2020 / arXiv:2006.10911"},{"authors":"Jialun Cao, David Siska, Lukasz Szpruch, Tanut Treetanthiploet","relevance":"Current market-making regret paper in a different Avellaneda-Stoikov stochastic-control model; useful adjacent context only.","title":"Logarithmic regret in the ergodic Avellaneda-Stoikov market making model","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.02025","venue_year":"SIAM Journal on Financial Mathematics 2026 / arXiv:2409.02025"},{"authors":"Jakob Albers, Mihai Cucuringu, Sam Howison, Alexander Y. Shestopaloff","relevance":"Empirical fill-probability versus post-fill-return tradeoff; adjacent market-microstructure motivation, not a delayed-fill regret characterization.","title":"The Market Maker's Dilemma: Navigating the Fill Probability vs. Post-Fill Returns Trade-Off","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18625","venue_year":"arXiv 2025, revised 2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"A hidden intermediate-horizon constraint or endpoint convention could force an extra term; strict-crossing implementation may need a separate eta-loss lemma.","suggested_lenses":["primal-dual verification","edge-case audit"],"summary":"Audit the solver's claimed formula for the two-level prefix h^M,0^N,l,0^L,h: D_n = [l(M+N)-h(M-1)_+ ]_+ (1-l/h). Check M=0, M=1, N=0, L=0, all post-low intermediate horizons, and the quote-at-low-time convention. If correct, promote it as the resolved post-low single-drought lemma.","title":"Verify Post-Low Drought Formula","why_promising":"It resolves the iteration-15 target and gives the cleanest local regression test for any two-level debt recursion."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Earlier low horizons may not be dominated by the final low; capacity constraints on where low-mass can be placed may make the LP more complex than the solver's sketch suggests.","suggested_lenses":["finite LP duality","cumulative scheduling"],"summary":"Generalize to one high-free episode with several low prices: h^M,0^{N_0},l,0^{N_1},l,...,0^{N_r},l,0^L,h. Formulate the exact LP with cumulative low-horizon surplus constraints S_j - l P_j and final high debt (h-l)P_total. Determine whether the last low alone binds or whether early lows create additional front-loading constraints.","title":"Multi-Low Single-Drought LP","why_promising":"This is the smallest nontrivial test of the proposed resolved-surplus/debt picture."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Small finite examples may mislead, and numerical LP output will still need exact rational or symbolic proof before promotion.","suggested_lenses":["computational experiment","counterexample search"],"summary":"Implement the formal left-limit finite-prefix LP for two-level paths and enumerate short paths over {0,l,h}, plus parametric repeated diluted droughts. Extract primal marginals and dual supports; compare D_n against the solver formula, the stable-high gap Q_T, and candidate debt recursions.","title":"Exact Two-Level LP Experiments","why_promising":"The gluing problem has repeatedly defeated intuition; exact finite-prefix computations can quickly falsify proposed recursions or reveal stable invariants."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Local drought costs may not add because the same quote marginals serve overlapping future horizons; old high history can damp later conflicts nonlocally.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","queueing analogy"],"summary":"Try to define a two-level resolved-surplus/debt recursion. A low-dominated drought creates surplus relative to old high history; assigning low quote mass reduces low regret but creates high debt at the next resolving high. Seek upper and lower bounds for D_n or fixed-envelope feasibility via a reflected-workload invariant.","title":"Resolved-Surplus Debt Recursion","why_promising":"It directly targets the current bottleneck: replacing the refuted raw Q_T localization by a state variable that remembers both surplus and future high debt."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The augmented LP may become a tautological restatement of the global fixed-envelope criterion unless the shadow objectives are restricted in a useful way.","suggested_lenses":["dual localization","boundary certificate"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that retains late shadow thresholds/horizon weights when splitting a dual certificate. Use the exact boundary identity to ask whether repeated boundary defects either produce comparable-scale D_n mass or are discharged by measurable future quote opportunities.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Localization","why_promising":"It directly addresses the known failure of charging boundary defect to the shorter real prefix."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Fresh quote opportunities after each low may always allow a path-aware policy to repair old conflicts; cumulative checks may make D_n linear after all.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","gluing stress test"],"summary":"Search for an infinite market-path separator between D_n=o(n) and the fixed-envelope criterion using repeated diluted droughts, with L_i much larger than N_i but N_i arranged so low-horizon demands recur. Calibrate each block using the solver's local formula; if no separator appears, prove gluing for this restricted class.","title":"Repeated Diluted-Drought Separator","why_promising":"Simple separated blocks failed because one old conflict is diluted; repeated diluted conflicts are the next plausible negative construction."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Branch observability and horizon-aware pseudo-regret can trivialize the construction; the transfer lemma requires exact same-prefix laws and probability-weighted accounting.","suggested_lenses":["stochastic counterexample","conditional transfer"],"summary":"Use rare branches carrying long multi-threshold or drought gadgets to formalize an essential-sup obstruction: beta or gamma remains large on rare histories, while expected regret contribution p_i N_i kappa_i is o(T). Compute the exact p_i,N_i,L_i conditions and state which learner quantifiers it refutes.","title":"Rare-Branch Hardness Calibration","why_promising":"It would cleanly separate uniform conditional tail failure from expected decision-relevant regret, strengthening the counterexample bank."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Existing delayed-reward literature is mostly not this suffix-horizon full-information model; discontinuous threshold rewards and exogeneity assumptions must be mapped carefully.","suggested_lenses":["literature translation","algorithmic sufficiency"],"summary":"Explore adaptive truncation/skipping algorithms for the finite-grid reduction using outstanding-observation or priority-queue ideas from delayed online learning. Aim for regret bounds in terms of skipped payoff-weighted unresolved fills rather than a fixed H or raw beta tail.","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"This is independent of the known-path gluing problem and may yield a cleaner positive theorem aligned with modern delayed-feedback terminology."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s main collapse argument is essentially correct under the intended oracle quantifiers: fixed exogenous price law \(P\), known horizon \(T\), pseudo-regret \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\cdot]\), and policies allowed to depend on both \(P\) and \(T\). Under that regime, the problem is vacuous: choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote in expectation and play it constantly, giving regret \(\le \varepsilon_T=o(T)\).
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:45:  \forall P,T,\varepsilon>0\ \exists \pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}: R_T^P(\pi)\le \varepsilon
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:49:- The use of `sup` and \(\varepsilon\)-optimizers is correct and avoids the known strict-crossing maximum-attainment pathology.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:360:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:363:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\). Proof: \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\), while \(\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h)\).
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:397:If policies may depend on both \(P\) and \(T\), and no computability/statistical-access restriction is imposed, then for every \(\varepsilon>0\) there is a deterministic constant-quote policy \(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}\) with \(R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le\varepsilon\). Choose an \(\varepsilon\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and play it every round. Thus every exogenous process is learnable in this oracle regime, independent of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta,\Gamma\). This does not apply to \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, endogenous/adaptive prices, horizon-oblivious policies, or one learner required to work over a class.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:409:Equivalently, an epsilon-comparator version gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\) using quotes \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\). This is a lower bound for a learner not knowing the continuation, not an instance-wise lower bound for a process-aware learner.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:597:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:920:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:925:\forall\epsilon>0,\ \beta_\epsilon(h)\to0.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:963:For every \(\varepsilon>0\), choose \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) with
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:965:V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\ge V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:967:The deterministic policy that posts \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) every round has pseudo-regret at most \(\varepsilon\). This uses the problem's \(\sup_a\mathbb E-\mathbb E\) regret, law and horizon awareness, and no computability constraint. Status: accepted.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:983:An epsilon version comparing to \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\). Status: accepted for universal/minimax lower-bound use.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:1167:Failed completely for the problem's pseudo-regret. If a policy may depend on both the exogenous law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), it can choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)=\mathbb E_P\sum_t r_t(a)\) and play it every round. This gives \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process, so no nontrivial tail condition can be necessary under this quantifier order. This failure relies on pseudo-regret, exogeneity, and horizon/law awareness.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:1171:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:1554:Notes: This uses \(0\in[0,1]\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), the specific witness disappears once all spike heights are below \(\epsilon\). If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions remain, a related supremum pathology remains but needs a separate admissible learner argument.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:1600:Choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and post it in every round. The learner's expected reward equals \(V_T^P(a_T)\), so its pseudo-regret is at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta_T,\Gamma_T\), or any other tail statistic. This is not an algorithmic result unless the process law and horizon are known and approximate optimization is allowed.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:1677:Zero atoms are also only a scaling device. If \(\Pr(Z=0)=1-q\) and \(\Pr(Z=1)=q\), then \(\rho(Z)=q\) and \(\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=0\). Treating the zero atom as consuming one of the \(m\) support points or collapsing \(0\) to \(\rho\) before conditioning on \(Z>0\) changes the problem being bounded.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:2028:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous policy-independent prices and the problem's pseudo-regret, if a learner may depend on both the law \(P\) and horizon \(T\), it can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)\) and get \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:2029:- Accepted the finite two-continuation lower bound: for \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner has regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation, using `sup` comparator or an epsilon-comparator variant.
./logs/iter_010_ideas.stderr.log:2060:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous prices and pseudo-regret, a law-and-horizon-aware learner can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote and get \(o(T)\) regret for every process.
./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:3:The assigned idea is sound and important: if “sublinear regret is possible” allows the learner to depend on both the process law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), the learning problem collapses. For every process and every horizon, a learner can simply play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed comparator quote at every round. This gives regret at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta\), \(\gamma\), or any tail condition.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:16:Because rewards are bounded by \(T\), \(V_T^{*,P}<\infty\). For every \(\varepsilon>0\), there exists \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\in[0,1]\) with
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:18:V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\ge V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:20:The deterministic policy that posts \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) at every time has expected reward exactly \(V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\), hence regret at most \(\varepsilon\).
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:27:For every price-process law \(P\), every horizon \(T\), and every \(\varepsilon>0\), there exists a deterministic constant-quote policy \(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}\) with
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:29:R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le \varepsilon.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:52:Let \(\varepsilon_T=1\), or any \(o(T)\) sequence. For each \(T\), pick \(a_T\) satisfying
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:54:V_T^P(a_T)\ge \sup_{a\in[0,1]}V_T^P(a)-\varepsilon_T.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:62:V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon_T.
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:64:Therefore \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\).
./iterations/iter_002/idea_01/explorer_01.md:66:This also handles the existing maximum-attainment issue: no max is needed, only a supremum and an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:51:It should be stated as a finite-prefix left-limit infimum theorem for deterministic exogenous paths. It also gives valid lower-bound certificates: if \(D_n(P)\ge \epsilon n\) infinitely often, then any horizon-oblivious expected-regret policy has \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge\epsilon\).
./logs/iter_007_solver.stderr.log:469:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
