./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2469:But the horizon-oblivious known-path policy \(a_t=1-\varepsilon_t\), with \(\frac1T\sum_{t\le T}\varepsilon_t\to0\), has \(o(T)\) regret. At even horizons it nearly matches the comparator \(1^-\) on all previous quotes. At odd horizons it loses only the same accumulated \(\varepsilon_t\)'s plus an \(O(1)\) boundary effect near the final \(1/2\). Thus the compact suffix-menu bound is sufficient only, not necessary.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3047:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous policy-independent prices and the problem's pseudo-regret, if a learner may depend on both the law \(P\) and horizon \(T\), it can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)\) and get \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3048:- Accepted the finite two-continuation lower bound: for \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner has regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation, using `sup` comparator or an epsilon-comparator variant.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3079:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous prices and pseudo-regret, a law-and-horizon-aware learner can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote and get \(o(T)\) regret for every process.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3746:- Refuted necessity of \(\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)=o(T)\). On the alternating path \(P_{2k-1}=1/2,\ P_{2k}=1\), the suffix-menu sum is linear, but quoting \(1-\varepsilon_t\) with vanishing average \(\varepsilon_t\) has \(o(T)\) regret.
./logs/iter_013_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3850:3. Dualize and compute finite-prefix examples to identify interpretable horizon-conflict certificates.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:34:**Resolved single-drought lemma.** The same proof should extend to a high-free episode ending in a resolving high, even with several \(\ell\)-prices inside the episode: the binding low horizon is the last low before the resolving high. Earlier lows impose prefix-cumulative low-mass constraints, but these are dominated by the final low-surplus constraint because the surplus grows with available low coordinates.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:36:This gives a cleaner local debt interpretation: a low-dominated drought creates surplus
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:43:This does not prove \(D_n=o(n)\Rightarrow\) existence of one \(o(T)\)-regret anytime policy. It only solves one local drought family.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:47:Local drought costs are not proved additive across many droughts. Shared marginals can couple separated episodes, and old high history can damp later conflicts.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:50:The diluted drought obstruction remains decisive:
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:71:Formalize the multi-low single-drought version as a cumulative low-mass scheduling LP.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:73:Then try a two-level global decomposition into resolved drought episodes, tracking only unresolved low surplus and high debt.
./iterations/iter_016/solver_attempt.md:75:Run finite-prefix LP experiments on repeated diluted droughts to test whether the proposed debt recursion predicts \(D_n\).
./iterations/iter_013/idea_01/explorer_01.md:48:If no global \(o(T)\)-regret marginal sequence exists, then for some \(\epsilon>0\) and arbitrarily large \(n\), there should be a dual horizon distribution \(\lambda\) supported on a dyadic annulus, say \(T\in[n/2,n]\), whose finite-prefix dual value is at least \(\epsilon n\). This would contradict \(D_n=o(n)\). This is the key unproved step.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md:58:with regret against \(\sup_a\), using epsilon comparators \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) to avoid strict-threshold nonattainment.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md:67:up to epsilon losses. This gives a deterministic known process with no anytime sublinear pseudo-regret policy.
./iterations/iter_003/idea_02/explorer_01.md:71:The finite proof is essentially complete. For epsilon comparators,
./iterations/iter_011/solver_attempt.md:29:Thus, using actual quotes \(s_C(X)-\varepsilon_t\) with summable \(\varepsilon_t\), a process-aware horizon-oblivious randomized policy satisfies for every horizon
./iterations/iter_011/solver_attempt.md:76:Strict crossing prevents exact attainment of \(c^-\); actual actions require \(\varepsilon_t\)-approximations with \(\sum_t\varepsilon_t<\infty\).
./iterations/iter_011/solver_attempt.md:110:The theorem should be stated as an infimum theorem under strict crossing, with explicit \(\varepsilon_t\)-implementation for actual quote actions.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:50:For a deterministic exogenous path, let \(C_t=\overline{\{M_{t,T}:T>t\}}\). For any error schedule \(\varepsilon_t\ge0\) with \(\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t=o(T)\), there is a path-aware, horizon-oblivious randomized policy such that for every horizon \(T\),
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:55:\sum_{t<T}\delta(C_t)+\sum_{t<T}\varepsilon_t.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:58:Proof: choose \(\nu_t\) with compact-menu deficit at most \(\delta(C_t)+\varepsilon_t/2\), sample \(X_t\sim\nu_t\), and play \(A_t=(1-\varepsilon_t/2)X_t\). Then for every \(c\in C_t\),
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:63:c-\delta(C_t)-\varepsilon_t.
./logs/iter_011_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:122:But the horizon-oblivious policy that always quotes \(1-\varepsilon_t\), with average \(\varepsilon_t\to0\), has \(o(T)\) regret: at even horizons it nearly matches the quote \(1^-\), and at odd horizons it loses only \(O(1)\) near the final \(1/2\).
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:39:No direct delayed-market-making theorem found that conflicts with the explorer’s claim. The relevant known results support the entropy side only: one-dimensional monotone classes have bracketing entropy of order \(1/\epsilon\), while the delayed-market-making \(1/e\) theorem remains internal to this run.
./iterations/iter_004/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Julius Graf, Thibaut Mastrolia","relevance":"Recent adjacent market-making work with closing-auction/terminal-liquidity effects; useful for terminology but not a direct delayed-fill characterization result.","title":"Learning Market Making with Closing Auctions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17247","venue_year":"arXiv:2601.17247, 2026"},{"authors":"Ofir Schlisselberg, Tal Lancewicki, Peter Auer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Modern delayed-bandit bounds matching lower bounds and emphasizing cumulative delay/missing observations, relevant to adaptive truncation alternatives.","title":"Improved Best-of-Both-Worlds Regret for Bandits with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24193","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2025 / arXiv:2505.24193"},{"authors":"Saeed Masoudian, Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Robust delayed-bandit algorithm using skipping and outstanding-observation counts; conceptually useful for replacing hard tail cutoffs by missing-information penalties.","title":"A Best-of-both-worlds Algorithm for Bandits with Delayed Feedback with Robustness to Excessive Delays","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=LDzrQB4X5w","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Robert E. Schapire","relevance":"Directly relevant background for unknown/adversarial horizon quantifiers and horizon-aware versus horizon-oblivious online learning.","title":"Towards Minimax Online Learning with Unknown Time Horizon","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/luo14.html","venue_year":"ICML 2014, PMLR 32(1):226-234"},{"authors":"Eyal Gofer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Studies individual-sequence anytime regret over prefixes; useful for positioning the same-path prefix-conflict lower bound.","title":"Lower bounds on individual sequence regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-015-5531-y","venue_year":"Machine Learning 103(1):1-26, 2016"},{"authors":"Cristobal Guzman, Nishant Mehta, Ali Mortazavi","relevance":"Clarifies best-case and anytime-prefix notions in online learning; useful for avoiding quantifier confusion in prefix lower bounds.","title":"Best-case lower bounds in online learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2021/hash/b7da6669894867f04b8727876a69ffc0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2021"},{"authors":"Shie Mannor, John N. Tsitsiklis, Jia Yuan Yu","relevance":"Sample-path constraint framework may help formulate simultaneous-prefix compatibility or approachability-style feasibility conditions.","title":"Online Learning with Sample Path Constraints","url":"https://jmlr.org/beta/papers/v10/mannor09a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 10:569-590, 2009"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Quantifier mistakes are the main risk: process-unaware, horizon-aware, randomized, and adaptive-adversary variants differ. Cross-block pending rewards and strict-threshold suprema also need careful epsilon accounting.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's concatenated universal block sketch into a theorem. For any randomized learner that does not know future continuations, choose dominant blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1}, M_i=o(N_i), and prove limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/4. Check the coupling after a fixed prefix, the old-reward cap by M_i, the eta-comparator version for strict crossing, and beta/gamma failure on the constructed sequence.","title":"Formalize Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"The finite two-continuation lemma and prefix-cap fragment are already accepted; this is the shortest path from solver sketch to a real class-uniform impossibility theorem."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The policy model must be exact: a process-aware policy may know the entire infinite path but not the evaluation horizon. Need ensure later blocks cannot retroactively affect regret at the paired endpoints.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","model definition","edge-case audit"],"summary":"Formalize the same-path horizon-oblivious theorem for the deterministic path made of blocks 0^{N_i}, 1/2, 1. Prove that for endpoints T_i^0=M_i+N_i+1 and T_i^1=M_i+N_i+2, any horizon-oblivious policy satisfies max{R_{T_i^0},R_{T_i^1}} >= N_i(1/4-o(1))-M_i-O(1).","title":"Known-Path Horizon-Oblivious Obstruction","why_promising":"It cleanly separates the horizon-aware oracle collapse from anytime learning, using one fixed deterministic process rather than a process class."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A family pi_T may be allowed to change its current-block behavior at each endpoint, so the infinite construction may require a diagonal or endpoint-specific adversarial choice. The result may only hold for oblivious adversaries or for fixed algorithms.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","proof strategy"],"summary":"Investigate whether the universal block lower bound extends to horizon-aware learner families pi_T that know T but not the continuation sequence. The finite two-continuation gadget has the same horizon under c=1/2 and c=1, suggesting an endpoint-wise construction may work; write or refute the diagonalization carefully.","title":"Horizon-Aware Universal Learners","why_promising":"Many regret statements allow the horizon as input. Settling this would make the lower-bound regime much clearer than the current anytime-only statement."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The quantity may become tautological, equivalent to minimax regret itself, or too hard to estimate. A useful definition must explain CE-001 as easy despite beta failure.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant prefix-incompatibility modulus: over pairs of continuations or same-path horizon pairs sharing a public prefix, take the smallest possible worst regret of any common-prefix action rule. Test whether finite blocks give modulus about N/4 while sparse vanishing spikes have small modulus despite beta(h)=1.","title":"Prefix-Incompatibility Tail Substitute","why_promising":"Current evidence says delay length is not the obstruction; incompatible optimal prefixes before resolving information are. A modulus of this kind could become the right necessity statement."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Positive-payoff rare branches may still create nonnegligible comparator value unless probabilities decay fast enough. Essential-sup tails require positive-probability branches at all large delays, and all-horizon regret bounds are easy to get wrong.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a complete rare-history process where beta or gamma fails by essential supremum on tiny positive-probability branches, while expected pseudo-regret is o(T) for a simple process-aware or universal learner. Compute both conditional tails and comparator values for all horizons.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Stress Test","why_promising":"It would show that even positive-scale conditional tail conditions can be too strong under expected pseudo-regret, strengthening the case for averaged or information-based quantities."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit/OCO rather than public-price full-information threshold rewards. The market-making reduction must specify exactly when each grid arm's reward is known and how skipped fills affect comparator regret.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","algorithm design","proof strategy"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping and outstanding-observation delayed-learning results to the grid market-making problem. Aim for a sufficient theorem that does not require a known H and is bounded by skipped payoff mass or value-weighted outstanding rewards, not by sup_h beta(h).","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"Recent delayed-feedback literature increasingly treats missing information, not elapsed time, as the complexity measure; this matches the counterexamples where long delays are predictable or irrelevant."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The delayed setting may still require many active breakpoints, and improved discretization will not address necessity. Strict crossing and atoms mean exact maximizers may still fail without left-limit conventions.","suggested_lenses":["structural analysis","algorithm design"],"summary":"Exploit the identity r_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H is the future-window maximum price. Study whether the continuum comparator can be handled exactly by intervals between observed prices, left limits, or adaptive grids, reducing or removing the T/K discretization term in the sufficiency theorem.","title":"Threshold Geometry For Continuum Quotes","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a cleaner continuum result and clarify sup-versus-max pathologies without relying on a crude uniform grid."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./iterations/iter_018/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Thibaut Vidal, Patrick Jaillet, Nelson Maculan","relevance":"Exact polynomial algorithms for resource allocation with nested partial-sum constraints; directly relevant to deriving a pooling or decomposition rule for the unequal-low reduced LP.","title":"A Decomposition Algorithm for Nested Resource Allocation Problems","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/140965119","venue_year":"SIAM Journal on Optimization 26(2):1322-1340, 2016"},{"authors":"Thibaut Vidal, Daniel Gribel, Patrick Jaillet","relevance":"Studies separable convex optimization with nested lower and upper partial-sum constraints, closer to the capacity and low-horizon inequality structure in the proposed drought LP.","title":"Separable Convex Optimization with Nested Lower and Upper Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/ijoo.2018.0004","venue_year":"INFORMS Journal on Optimization 1(1):71-90, 2019"},{"authors":"Jan de Leeuw, Kurt Hornik, Patrick Mair","relevance":"Generalized PAVA and active-set methods for isotone optimization; useful proof technology for a possible pooling rule in the unequal-low dual.","title":"Isotone Optimization in R: Pool-Adjacent-Violators Algorithm (PAVA) and Active Set Methods","url":"https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v032.i05","venue_year":"Journal of Statistical Software 32(5):1-24, 2009"},{"authors":"Ravindra K. Ahuja, James B. Orlin","relevance":"Fast algorithms for separable convex objectives under chain constraints; useful background if the reduced LP can be transformed into a generalized isotonic or chain-constrained problem.","title":"A Fast Scaling Algorithm for Minimizing Separable Convex Functions Subject to Chain Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.49.5.784.10601","venue_year":"Operations Research 49(5):784-789, 2001"},{"authors":"Zeyang Wu, Kameng Nip, Qie He","relevance":"Combinatorial algorithms for nested bound resource allocation, including infeasibility-guided divide-and-conquer and submodular-constraint ideas that may inform finite-prefix LP algorithms.","title":"A New Combinatorial Algorithm for Separable Convex Resource Allocation with Nested Bound Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/ijoc.2020.1006","venue_year":"INFORMS Journal on Computing 33(3):1197-1212, 2021"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The rounding-to-low-levels dominance may fail at a strict-crossing boundary, the old-high subtraction S_j=V^*_{s_j}-hH may miss a comparator regime, or intermediate zero horizons may create constraints not represented by the low horizons.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","strict-crossing cleanup","finite-prefix LP duality"],"summary":"Prove or disprove the solver's full-to-reduced LP reduction for one unequal-low drought. Start from the accepted finite-prefix D_n primal in the formal left-limit model, partition quote coordinates into protected old-high, useful segment, and final-only classes, justify the action menu L union {h}, derive the low-horizon constraints and final-high debt constraint, and show all intermediate zero horizons are dominated by the latest low constraint.","title":"Audit Unequal-Low LP Reduction","why_promising":"This is the immediate bottleneck in iteration 18. A clean proof promotes the candidate LP to an accepted local theorem; a failure gives a precise counterexample to the solver attempt."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The displayed dual may be only the dual of the reduced LP, not of the full finite-prefix LP; dual mass on intermediate horizons could improve the certificate; low-level menus may need running maxima not included in L.","suggested_lenses":["dual certificate","LP verification","counterexample search"],"summary":"Attack the same theorem from the finite-prefix dual. Restrict dual support to low horizons and the final high, compute the per-segment support terms, and verify that they match the solver's psi_i expression. Then ask whether any omitted horizon can increase the full dual objective in one-drought paths.","title":"Dualize The Drought LP","why_promising":"The solver's dual formula is compact and may be the cleanest route to a lower bound. It also directly checks the final-debt term and the S_j normalization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The reduced LP may be a generic piecewise-linear program with no clean pooling law; nonmonotone lows can destroy the chain structure that PAVA relies on; active-set formulas may become more complex than useful.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","KKT conditions","nested resource allocation"],"summary":"Assuming the reduced LP is exact, derive a pooling, PAVA-like, or nested-resource-allocation algorithm from its dual. Use complementary slackness to identify active low constraints, active segment levels c, and the beta/alpha tradeoff between low payoff and final high debt. Regression-test on two- and three-low examples, including nonmonotone low sequences.","title":"Pooling Rule For Unequal Lows","why_promising":"A structural solver would turn the local LP into a usable surplus/debt rule and might reveal the right state variable for repeated droughts."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Finite experiments may pass without yielding insight; numerical LP tolerances can hide strict-crossing or left-limit issues; exhaustive search grows quickly with path length and alphabet size.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite LP computation","rational certificates"],"summary":"Build a small exact finite-prefix LP regression suite. For grids of lows such as {0.25,0.5,0.75}, enumerate one-drought paths with small M, N_i, L, compute D_n from LB-031, compute the proposed reduced LP, and compare primal/dual certificates. Prioritize minimal failing examples, then extend to repeated droughts.","title":"Exact LP Regression Suite","why_promising":"This gives a precise independent check of the solver's proposed reduction and can quickly find the first missing constraint or indexing error."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Single-drought formulas may not compose; the necessary state may be a high-dimensional vector of level-specific debts; experiments may only show dilution without a theorem.","suggested_lenses":["gluing","surplus-debt recursion","finite-alphabet paths"],"summary":"Study two or more unequal-low droughts sharing future highs. Compare the full finite-prefix value with sums or compositions of the single-drought LP. Track how low-level quote mass creates persistent high debt and whether debt can be summarized by level, by segment, or by a scalar workload.","title":"Repeated-Drought High Debt","why_promising":"The notes identify repeated droughts and cumulative high debt as the main remaining route toward the D_n=o(n) gluing problem. This direction tests whether the new local LP is composable."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The augmented certificate may become tautological or too close to the full D_n dual; separated-block examples already show naive boundary charging fails, so a useful localization theorem needs extra structure.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","boundary defect","gluing theorem"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that keeps late shadow thresholds in the local certificate. Use the one-drought LP as a model: lows are local horizons, the resolving high is a shadow objective creating debt. Explore whether repeated augmented boundary defects either force comparable-scale D_n or are exactly absorbed by later quote opportunities.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Localization","why_promising":"This addresses the known failure of direct dual localization while preserving the information that made separated and drought examples analyzable."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_009_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
