./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:679:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:801:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:804:Then \(\gamma(h)\to0\) iff \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\to0\) for every \(\epsilon>0\). Proof: \(\beta_\epsilon(h)\le \gamma(h)/\epsilon\), while \(\gamma(h)\le \epsilon+\beta_\epsilon(h)\).
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:838:If policies may depend on both \(P\) and \(T\), and no computability/statistical-access restriction is imposed, then for every \(\varepsilon>0\) there is a deterministic constant-quote policy \(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon}\) with \(R_T^P(\pi_{P,T,\varepsilon})\le\varepsilon\). Choose an \(\varepsilon\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and play it every round. Thus every exogenous process is learnable in this oracle regime, independent of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta,\Gamma\). This does not apply to \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, endogenous/adaptive prices, horizon-oblivious policies, or one learner required to work over a class.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:850:Equivalently, an epsilon-comparator version gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\) using quotes \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\). This is a lower bound for a learner not knowing the continuation, not an instance-wise lower bound for a process-aware learner.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1083:\beta_\epsilon(h)=\sup_t\sup_{a\in[\epsilon,1]}\operatorname*{ess\,sup}_{\mathcal F_t}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1088:\forall\epsilon>0,\ \beta_\epsilon(h)\to0.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1126:For every \(\varepsilon>0\), choose \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) with
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1128:V_T^P(a_{T,\varepsilon})\ge V_T^{*,P}-\varepsilon.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1130:The deterministic policy that posts \(a_{T,\varepsilon}\) every round has pseudo-regret at most \(\varepsilon\). This uses the problem's \(\sup_a\mathbb E-\mathbb E\) regret, law and horizon awareness, and no computability constraint. Status: accepted.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1146:An epsilon version comparing to \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\). Status: accepted for universal/minimax lower-bound use.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1812:Failed completely for the problem's pseudo-regret. If a policy may depend on both the exogenous law \(P\) and the horizon \(T\), it can choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)=\mathbb E_P\sum_t r_t(a)\) and play it every round. This gives \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process, so no nontrivial tail condition can be necessary under this quantifier order. This failure relies on pseudo-regret, exogeneity, and horizon/law awareness.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1816:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1884:Notes: This uses \(0\in[0,1]\). If actions are restricted to \([\epsilon,1]\), the specific witness disappears once all spike heights are below \(\epsilon\). If \(0\) is excluded but arbitrarily small actions remain, a related supremum pathology remains but needs a separate admissible learner argument.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:1930:Choose an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimizer of \(\sup_a V_T^P(a)\) and post it in every round. The learner's expected reward equals \(V_T^P(a_T)\), so its pseudo-regret is at most \(\varepsilon_T=o(T)\), regardless of \(\beta,\gamma,\Delta_T,\Gamma_T\), or any other tail statistic. This is not an algorithmic result unless the process law and horizon are known and approximate optimization is allowed.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2007:Zero atoms are also only a scaling device. If \(\Pr(Z=0)=1-q\) and \(\Pr(Z=1)=q\), then \(\rho(Z)=q\) and \(\mathbb EZ-\rho(Z)=0\). Treating the zero atom as consuming one of the \(m\) support points or collapsing \(0\) to \(\rho\) before conditioning on \(Z>0\) changes the problem being bounded.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2527:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous policy-independent prices and the problem's pseudo-regret, if a learner may depend on both the law \(P\) and horizon \(T\), it can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for \(V_T^P(a)\) and get \(R_T^P\le\varepsilon_T=o(T)\) for every process.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2528:- Accepted the finite two-continuation lower bound: for \(0^N c\), \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\), every randomized learner has regret at least \(N/4\) on one continuation, using `sup` comparator or an epsilon-comparator variant.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:2559:- Promoted the horizon/process-aware collapse: under exogenous prices and pseudo-regret, a law-and-horizon-aware learner can play an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote and get \(o(T)\) regret for every process.
