./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3327:./logs/iter_002_ideas.stdout.log:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Andras Gyorgy, Pooria Joulani","relevance":"Useful for adaptive delay/skipping bounds that may replace a fixed truncation horizon in the grid market-making reduction.","title":"Adapting to Delays and Data in Adversarial Multi-Armed Bandits","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/gyorgy21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 139, 2021"},{"authors":"Genevieve E. Flaspohler, Francesco Orabona, Judah Cohen, Soukayna Mouatadid, Miruna Oprescu, Paulo Orenstein, Lester Mackey","relevance":"Gives a delay-as-optimism perspective that may help exploit predictable delayed fills rather than charging all long delays as tail loss.","title":"Online Learning with Optimism and Delay","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/flaspohler21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 139, 2021"},{"authors":"Paul Duetting, Guru Guruganesh, Jon Schneider, Joshua Ruizhi Wang","relevance":"Potential tool for exploiting the one-sided threshold geometry of fixed-quote payoff curves and improving naive grid discretization.","title":"Optimal No-Regret Learning for One-Sided Lipschitz Functions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/duetting23b.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 202, 2023"},{"authors":"Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Claudio Gentile, Yishay Mansour, Alberto Minora","relevance":"Contains delayed-bandit minimax background and lower-bound methodology relevant to the two-continuation block lower bound.","title":"Delay and Cooperation in Nonstochastic Bandits","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v49/cesa-bianchi16.html","venue_year":"COLT / PMLR 49, 2016"},{"authors":"Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari","relevance":"Full-information delayed OCO black-box reductions may support a cleaner proof of the finite-grid sufficiency theorem and horizon-free variants.","title":"Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization: Unified Analysis and Adaptive-Gradient Algorithms","url":"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/10320","venue_year":"AAAI, 2016"},{"authors":"Robert D. Kleinberg, Frank Thomson Leighton","relevance":"Foundational posted-price regret lower bounds; relevant to testing whether the quote-payoff threshold geometry admits sharper discretization or lower bounds.","title":"The Value of Knowing a Demand Curve: Bounds on Regret for Online Posted-Price Auctions","url":"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/946243.946352","venue_year":"FOCS, 2003"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"This may mainly clarify definitions rather than produce a new theorem; horizon-oblivious process-aware learnability may still collapse under a different oracle argument.","suggested_lenses":["definition audit","proof strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Formalize the solver's quantifier-collapse observation: if the learner may depend on both the process law and the horizon, every process is learnable by repeatedly playing a horizon-optimal fixed quote up to an epsilon. Then separate the nontrivial regimes: horizon-oblivious process-aware learners, universal learners over a class, minimax learners, and post-history uniform guarantees.","title":"Pin Down The Nontrivial Quantifiers","why_promising":"It may completely settle which readings of the original iff are impossible before explorers spend effort proving necessity statements that are vacuous or false."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The lower bound may only prove a universal-class or minimax impossibility, not an instance-wise characterization; strict crossing and unattained suprema need careful epsilon handling.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao/minimax","strict-threshold audit"],"summary":"Turn the two-continuation block example into a theorem. Use N initial zeros followed by a terminal price in {1/2, 1}; before the terminal observation the learner cannot know whether quotes below 1/2 or near 1 are needed. Prove that for any randomized learner one continuation has Omega(N) regret, then concatenate superexponentially growing blocks to get a class-level no-sublinear-regret result.","title":"Two-Continuation Indistinguishability Lower Bound","why_promising":"This is the most concrete route from raw tail failure to a real hardness theorem, and it directly targets the information-theoretic obstruction identified in the notes."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A too-exact functional could become tautological or unobservable; proving both sufficiency and necessity may require strong class assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["information theory","functional design","proof strategy"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant delayed-tail quantity that counts unresolved payoff only when it can change the identity or value of the best fixed quote conditional on the current history. Possible forms include a conditional value-of-information gap, a Le Cam two-continuation modulus, or a supremum over pairs of continuations that are observationally identical for h steps but have separated optimal quotes.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Mass","why_promising":"All accepted counterexamples show that raw beta, gamma, and Delta fail because delayed mass is tiny, predictable, or irrelevant. A value-of-information tail is aligned with the actual obstruction."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction must keep expected comparator value small while maintaining positive-payoff essential-sup tails; if the rare branch is fully coded, learnability becomes trivial and the example may overlap CE-003.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","horizon calculations","essential-sup audit"],"summary":"Build a rare-branch process with positive-probability histories containing arbitrarily long profitable delays, so beta or gamma fails by essential supremum, while the ex-ante contribution to regret is summable or o(T). A candidate is to draw a rare branch m with probability p_m, reveal or partially reveal a code, wait L_m steps, and then emit a constant-scale random terminal price with p_m L_m summable.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Stress Test","why_promising":"It would show that uniform post-history tail conditions are too strong even at positive payoff scale, and would motivate expectation, quantile, or predictable-tail alternatives."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"These quantities are horizon-dependent and may still be far from necessary for learnability; they may mostly improve sufficiency rather than characterize possibility.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","comparison inequalities","counterexample calculations"],"summary":"Develop the hierarchy between Delta_T(H), the smaller comparator gap Gamma_T(H)=sup_a V_T(a)-sup_a V_T^H(a), and possible argmax-stability gaps. Prove the clean regret comparison R_T <= R_T^H + Gamma_T(H), compute Gamma on CE-001/CE-003/CE-005, and identify when Gamma_T(H_T)=o(T) is enough for truncation-based algorithms.","title":"Sharper Truncation Gap Hierarchy","why_promising":"It gives an immediate sharper sufficient condition and may reveal exactly which part of delayed payoff mass matters to fixed-comparator regret."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit or OCO results, while the market-making reduction is full-information with arm-dependent and infinite non-fill delays; the mapping may require a new wrapper rather than direct citation.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","literature mapping","model reduction"],"summary":"Translate adaptive-delay and skipping algorithms to the finite grid market-making model. Treat unresolved fills after a chosen cutoff as skipped observations and aim for regret bounded by learning terms plus skipped payoff mass, without knowing beta, gamma, or a good H in advance. Compare residue-class Hedge, Joulani-Gyorgy-Szepesvari reductions, Gyorgy-Joulani skipping, and optimism-with-delay methods.","title":"Adaptive Skipping For Grid Market Making","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a more usable algorithm and connect predictable long delays to optimism rather than worst-case truncation loss."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Adversarial time variation and delayed observation may destroy the strong one-sided-Lipschitz guarantees; naive T/K discretization may be minimax-tight for the delayed adversarial problem.","suggested_lenses":["threshold geometry","adaptive discretization","lower bound"],"summary":"Exploit that each truncated reward is a threshold function r_t^H(a)=a 1{a < M_t^H}. Study whether cumulative payoff curves have one-sided Lipschitz or posted-price structure that permits adaptive grids, binary-search-style localization, or smaller discretization regret than T/K. Cross-check against Kleinberg-Leighton and Duetting-Guruganesh-Schneider-Wang style lower bounds.","title":"Threshold Geometry Beyond Uniform Grids","why_promising":"The current proof treats quotes as unrelated arms after discretization, losing structure that may be crucial for sharp rates or for a cleaner characterization."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"This is mostly negative progress unless paired with a positive characterization; all-horizon bounds can be delicate near block boundaries.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample formalization","all-horizon proof","critic audit"],"summary":"Polish the main counterexamples into standalone propositions: sparse vanishing spikes refuting beta-necessity, coded delays refuting gamma-necessity, and predictable spikes refuting Delta-necessity. For CE-005, finish the all-horizons proof for the superexponential uniform-spike variant and decide whether the binary power-of-two variant requires horizon-aware actions.","title":"Counterexample Package With Exact Quantifiers","why_promising":"A rigorous negative package will prevent future agents from re-proving false necessity claims and will clarify exactly which assumptions any revised theorem must include."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3329:./working_notes/canonical_summary.md:5:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3386:./logs/iter_002_idea_01_explorer_01_critic_01.stdout.log:49:- The use of `sup` and \(\varepsilon\)-optimizers is correct and avoids the known strict-crossing maximum-attainment pathology.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3439:./iterations/iter_009/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Randomized minimax-regret pricing with only support information is the closest continuous-pricing analogue of the solver's primal randomized quote law.","title":"Pricing Without Priors","url":"https://doi.org/10.1162/JEEA.2008.6.2-3.560","venue_year":"Journal of the European Economic Association, 2008"},{"authors":"Dirk Bergemann and Karl H. Schlag","relevance":"Robust monopoly-pricing minimax regret source emphasizing that randomized pricing can be optimal under regret criteria; useful context for the finite-C primal game.","title":"Robust Monopoly Pricing","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jet.2011.10.018","venue_year":"Journal of Economic Theory, 2011"},{"authors":"Rene Caldentey, Ying Liu, and Ilan Lobel","relevance":"Dynamic/intertemporal minimax-regret pricing may supply terminology or techniques for horizon-oblivious known-path upper bounds.","title":"Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.2016.1548","venue_year":"Operations Research, 2017"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang, Shaoxuan Liu, and Jiawei Zhang","relevance":"Finite-support LP and randomized-pricing implementation results are adjacent to the finite threshold-set kappa primal-dual program.","title":"Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/msom.2023.0072","venue_year":"Manufacturing & Service Operations Management, 2024"},{"authors":"Shixin Wang","relevance":"Finite-menu randomized selling mechanisms are a useful comparison point for finite action distributions q_i in the local threshold game.","title":"The Power of Simple Menus in Robust Selling Mechanisms","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17392","venue_year":"Management Science / arXiv, 2024-2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","literature audit","finite examples"],"summary":"Verify and package the solver's finite-C primal formula. For C={0<c_1<...<c_m}, check that the unique root delta* of F(delta)=1 really equals kappa(C), including m=1, delta*=0, delta*=c_i boundary cases, and left-limit actions. Then compare the q_i distribution to randomized minimax pricing formulas in Bergemann-Schlag-style prior-free pricing.","title":"Promote Finite-C Primal Formula","why_promising":"This directly closes the iteration-8 open item and gives an explicit local optimizer, not just a dual value. It can become a reusable lemma for both lower bounds and candidate upper bounds."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"Local block strategies may not compose because one fixed comparator spans multiple blocks and old quotes can fill at later records. Nonmonotone price paths may break the clean threshold-menu reduction.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","dynamic programming","counterexample search"],"summary":"Try for a matching known-path anytime upper bound using the explicit local minimax mixtures. Model a deterministic known path by epochs whose future record thresholds form finite menus, choose q_i-style quote distributions on long unresolved stretches, and test whether every path admits limsup_T R_T/T <= 1/e or a sharper path-dependent local-density bound.","title":"Known-Path Anytime Upper Bound","why_promising":"The same-path lower bound now reaches 1/e, and the solver supplies the primal strategy that matches each isolated block. If these strategies compose, the process-aware horizon-oblivious regime could get a sharp characterization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Approachability may become infinite-dimensional as thresholds and horizons grow; feedback is horizon-censored; global comparator values may not decompose into local vector payoffs.","suggested_lenses":["approachability","algorithm design","finite-to-continuum limit"],"summary":"Formulate horizon-oblivious learning on a known path as vector-deficit approachability. For a finite active threshold set C, define deficit coordinates against each prefix/comparator and use the solver's q_i as the one-shot minimax action. Explore whether Blackwell approachability or global-cost regret gives an anytime policy when the target set is approachable.","title":"Vector Deficit Approachability","why_promising":"It turns the informal 'one quote sequence serves many horizons' obstruction into a standard simultaneous-objective problem and may produce both algorithms and impossibility certificates."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The construction can accidentally become hard in expectation if rare blocks are not predictable enough, or trivial if a process-aware learner can condition on the branch. Must avoid presenting the dual threshold law itself as the hard stochastic process.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","quantifier audit","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a rare-event process where, conditional on E_i, a long zero block is followed by an equal-revenue threshold menu with kappa close to 1/e, but P(E_i)N_i=o(T_i). Check that essential-sup tails and conditional local kappa stay large while expected regret remains o(T) for a process-aware or suitably informed learner.","title":"Rare Event Kappa Stress Test","why_promising":"This would strengthen CE-006 from a suggestion into a formal obstruction against any necessity condition based on essential sup or unweighted post-history hardness."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"The best truncation gap Gamma_T(H) may be unobservable; meta-learning across H has delayed/censored feedback; most arbitrary-delay literature is bandit/OCO rather than this full-information public-price reduction.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","delayed-feedback literature mapping","oracle inequality"],"summary":"Develop an oracle-free sufficiency theorem that competes with the best truncation scale H in hindsight: run multi-scale truncated grid learners, skip fills unresolved after H, and aim for regret roughly inf_H {sqrt(T H log K)+Gamma_T(H)} plus meta-overhead and T/K.","title":"Adaptive Truncation Oracle Bound","why_promising":"The current sufficiency theorem needs a chosen H or tail rate. An adaptive skipping result would give a stronger positive theorem aligned with the modern delayed-feedback literature on skipped or outstanding observations."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"A broad modulus may become tautological or noncomputable. Same-prefix action laws fail for process-aware horizon-aware learners, and outside-surplus compensation can erase local gaps.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound framework","stress tests"],"summary":"Define a probability-weighted information obstruction, e.g. Exposure_T = sup over common-prefix events/candidate horizons of p|I|kappa_I - B, where B caps outside-prefix surplus. Prove the conditional transfer lemma gives lower bounds in universal or horizon-oblivious regimes, then test against sparse spikes, coded delays, rare events, and equal-revenue blocks.","title":"Decision-Relevant Tail Modulus","why_promising":"It targets the real obstruction identified by the notes: frequency, unpredictability, payoff relevance, and inability to compensate, rather than raw delay length."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"Mostly technical and may not create new mathematics; losses can grow with m if thresholds are close or if diagonal menus are mishandled.","suggested_lenses":["proof hygiene","epsilon cleanup","theorem polishing"],"summary":"Create a reusable strict-crossing cleanup lemma: replace left-limit quotes a up to c_i by actual quotes c_i-eta_i, quantify the loss in N-block and multi-block theorems, and state exactly when sup comparators remove the need for eta. Include finite-grid versus continuum action-domain conversions.","title":"Strict-Crossing Cleanup Toolkit","why_promising":"Many accepted lower bounds and the solver's primal formula rely on left limits. A clean technical package would reduce repeated caveats in the final writeup."},{"id":"idea_08","risks":"Running maxima do not capture rewards of quotes posted after downward moves; multiplicities and single-fill interactions may require more state than a threshold set.","suggested_lenses":["deterministic-path analysis","counterexample search","weighted threshold LP"],"summary":"Extend the same-path multi-threshold theorem beyond increasing blocks. For arbitrary threshold order, rewrite zero-block fill events through running maxima and derive the correct weighted or repeated-threshold kappa game. Identify whether nonmonotone blocks reduce to record values or create new local obstructions.","title":"Nonmonotone Threshold Blocks","why_promising":"Real known paths are not sorted threshold menus. This is a necessary step from block gadgets toward a deterministic-path anytime characterization."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3442:./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:109:and the learner can match the best expected fixed action up to epsilon. The randomization over continuations is a Yao/minimax proof device for deterministic instances, not a hard stochastic process under the current regret definition.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3443:./logs/iter_002_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:113:Strict crossing means comparator maxima are unattained at \(1/2\) and \(1\). Use `sup` in the theorem, or use the epsilon-comparator version.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3554:./logs/iter_001_ideas.stderr.log:1020:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Pooria Joulani, Andras Gyorgy, Csaba Szepesvari","relevance":"Primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction for online learning; supports the solver's finite-grid truncated expert upper bound.","title":"Online Learning under Delayed Feedback","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v28/joulani13.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2013"},{"authors":"Kent Quanrud, Daniel Khashabi","relevance":"Gives full-information adversarial-delay regret in terms of cumulative delay, relevant to replacing a fixed truncation horizon by delay-adaptive bounds.","title":"Online Learning with Adversarial Delays","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/hash/72da7fd6d1302c0a159f6436d01e9eb0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2015"},{"authors":"Tobias Sommer Thune, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Introduces unrestricted-delay bandit techniques and skipping of excessively delayed feedback, a likely template for sharper market-making conditions.","title":"Nonstochastic Multiarmed Bandits with Unrestricted Delays","url":"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/8881-nonstochastic-multiarmed-bandits-with-unrestricted-delays","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2019"},{"authors":"Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined skip-set guarantees; useful terminology for excessive-delay robustness.","title":"An Optimal Algorithm for Adversarial Bandits with Arbitrary Delays","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.06054","venue_year":"AISTATS 2020"},{"authors":"Andras Gyorgy, Pooria Joulani","relevance":"Delay- and data-adaptive adversarial bandit bounds, including skipping; may inspire bounds depending on delayed payoff mass rather than unweighted delay probability.","title":"Adapting to Delays and Data in Adversarial Multi-Armed Bandits","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.06022","venue_year":"ICML 2021"},{"authors":"Tal Lancewicki, Shahar Segal, Tomer Koren, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Uses delay quantiles and permits infinite delays in stochastic bandits; relevant to replacing uniform tail assumptions by quantile/truncation penalties.","title":"Stochastic Multi-Armed Bandits with Unrestricted Delay Distributions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/lancewicki21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Genevieve E. Flaspohler, Francesco Orabona, Judah Cohen, Soukayna Mouatadid, Miruna Oprescu, Paulo Orenstein, Lester Mackey","relevance":"Develops optimistic delayed online-learning algorithms and reductions, potentially useful for tuning-free full-information expert implementations.","title":"Online Learning with Optimism and Delay","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v139/flaspohler21a.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2021"},{"authors":"Paul Duetting, Guru Guruganesh, Jon Schneider, Joshua Ruizhi Wang","relevance":"One-sided Lipschitz maximization is structurally close to threshold/posting-price reward curves and may help improve discretization or exploit monotonicity.","title":"Optimal No-Regret Learning for One-Sided Lipschitz Functions","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v202/duetting23b.html","venue_year":"ICML / PMLR 2023"},{"authors":"Lukas Zierahn, Dirk van der Hoeven, Tal Lancewicki, Aviv Rosenberg, Nicolo Cesa-Bianchi","relevance":"Recent unified delayed-feedback analysis for nonstochastic bandit settings; useful for modern delay decomposition and total-delay terminology.","title":"A Unified Analysis of Nonstochastic Delayed Feedback for Combinatorial Semi-Bandits, Linear Bandits, and MDPs","url":"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v26/24-0496.html","venue_year":"JMLR 2025"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"If the intended theorem is minimax over a class of processes rather than literal per-process learnability, the sparse-spike counterexample may only expose a quantifier mismatch.","suggested_lenses":["proof audit","quantifier analysis","counterexample generalization"],"summary":"Verify the solver's sparse vanishing-spike refutation line by line: beta(h)=1 via action a=0 at spike times, the best fixed comparator has expected payoff at most 1 for every horizon, and the continuum maximum exists. Then try to strengthen it to a family with beta=1 and regret bounded by any prescribed sublinear envelope.","title":"Audit the Refutation","why_promising":"This is the shortest path to a rigorous conclusion about the stated iff: the unweighted tail condition appears to count long delays at actions with negligible payoff."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The essential supremum over histories may still be too strong; rare histories or isolated times can violate gamma without causing linear expected regret.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","condition refinement","counterexample search"],"summary":"Replace beta by the payoff-weighted tail gamma(h)=sup_t sup_a esssup a Pr(h<tau_t(a)<infty | history). Prove the same upper bound with T gamma(H) in place of T beta(H), and show equivalence to uniform tightness over positive-scale actions a>=epsilon for every epsilon>0. Then test whether gamma(h)->0 is necessary or only sufficient.","title":"Payoff-Weighted Tail","why_promising":"It directly repairs the solver's obstruction: long delays at a=0 or tiny quote levels should not matter for regret."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"Necessity may fail because an algorithm can exploit predictable price structure rather than waiting for delayed reward feedback.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","necessity test","rare-history examples"],"summary":"Study the ex-ante horizon-level truncation error Delta_T(H)=sup_a sum_{t<=T} E[a 1{H<tau_t(a)<=T-t}]. Establish sufficiency when some H_T has H_T log T=o(T) and Delta_T(H_T)=o(T), using the same delayed-grid reduction. Investigate whether this is closer to a true characterization than uniform gamma.","title":"Horizon Tail Error","why_promising":"This is the weakest condition visible from the truncation proof and avoids beta's worst-case sup over irrelevant times and histories."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Failure of beta alone is too weak for lower bounds; any theorem will likely need positive payoff scale, positive density in time, and unpredictability assumptions.","suggested_lenses":["lower bound","Yao construction","indistinguishability"],"summary":"If the intended statement is uniform over a class of processes, formulate a lower-bound condition based on indistinguishable blocks: two laws share the same observed prefix for H rounds but differ in whether a valuable quote crosses after the delay. Use Yao or Le Cam to force regret from actions chosen before the delayed signal arrives.","title":"Minimax Necessity","why_promising":"A defensible only-if theorem must convert meaningful long-tail mass into information-theoretic hardness; this direction isolates the needed quantifiers."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Naively summing delays over all grid arms may be far too pessimistic; the explorer must exploit full-information and nested-threshold structure.","suggested_lenses":["algorithmic reduction","known delayed-learning bounds","proof strategy"],"summary":"Replace fixed-H truncation with arbitrary-delay or skipping delayed-expert algorithms. For grid actions, reveal each reward when its crossing occurs or at horizon, and derive regret in terms of total counted delay, outstanding observations, or weighted skipped payoff. Compare the resulting condition to beta, gamma, and Delta_T.","title":"Skipping Bounds","why_promising":"Modern delayed-learning theory already handles excessive and unbounded delays; translating skip-set bounds may yield a sharper sufficient condition than uniform tail decay."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Existing one-sided Lipschitz or pricing results often assume immediate or stochastic feedback, so adapting them may not prove the delayed theorem directly.","suggested_lenses":["structural analysis","adaptive discretization","literature comparison"],"summary":"Exploit the deterministic identity r_t(a)=a 1{max_{s=t+1..T} P_s > a}; the fixed-action payoff is a times the last crossing time of level a. Investigate adaptive grids, one-sided Lipschitz optimization, and threshold nesting to improve the T/K discretization and identify the real obstruction to no-regret.","title":"Threshold Geometry","why_promising":"The market-making problem is more structured than generic delayed experts: one revealed price resolves an interval of quotes, and comparator payoffs are monotone threshold curves."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The outcome will depend heavily on the exact meaning of 'possible' and on whether the learner may depend on the process law.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite-case calculations","condition stress test"],"summary":"Systematically test proposed necessary conditions against constructed processes: rare high-value delayed spikes, deterministic predictable sparse spikes, vanishing-value dense spikes, and isolated bad histories. For each, compute beta, gamma, Delta_T, best fixed comparator payoff, and regret of simple learners.","title":"Stress-Test Conditions","why_promising":"This can prevent replacing the false unweighted beta condition with another overly strong condition before a lower-bound theorem is attempted."}],"solution_status":"maybe"}
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3570:./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:294:The stated "only if" direction is false under literal instance-wise/process-aware readings. The sparse vanishing-spike process \(P_{2^n}=U_n/2^n\), \(P_t=0\) otherwise, has \(\beta(h)=1\) for every \(h\) via \(a=0\), but the always-zero learner has regret at most \(1\) for every horizon. Stronger: if the learner may depend on both the exogenous process law and the horizon, and regret is the problem's pseudo-regret \(\sup_a\mathbb E[\cdot]-\mathbb E[\cdot]\), every process is trivially learnable by playing an \(\varepsilon_T\)-optimal fixed quote for that horizon. This collapse does not apply to endogenous prices, \(E[\sup]\) pathwise regret, horizon-oblivious learners, or universal/minimax guarantees.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3591:./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:779:The formula may be a known robust-pricing special case rather than new; boundary cases delta*=c_i and strict crossing could hide epsilon losses; it remains purely local even if correct.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3604:./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1053:An epsilon version comparing to \(1/2-\eta\) and \(1-\eta\) gives \(N(1/4-\eta)\). Status: accepted for universal/minimax lower-bound use.
./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:3635:./logs/iter_009_idea_01_explorer_01.stderr.log:1694:Failed under the current expected-comparator regret definition. Randomizing the terminal continuation \(c\in\{1/2,1\}\) is useful as a minimax/Yao-style proof device for deterministic instances, but the stochastic mixture itself may be easy because the comparator is \(\sup_a \mathbb E[\text{reward of }a]\). A learner can often match the best expected fixed quote up to epsilon. The block construction should be stated as a universal-class or minimax lower bound, not as an instance-wise stochastic-process lower bound.
