./logs/iter_010_idea_02_explorer_01.stderr.log:6289:./iterations/iter_004/promising_ideas.json:1:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Julius Graf, Thibaut Mastrolia","relevance":"Recent adjacent market-making work with closing-auction/terminal-liquidity effects; useful for terminology but not a direct delayed-fill characterization result.","title":"Learning Market Making with Closing Auctions","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.17247","venue_year":"arXiv:2601.17247, 2026"},{"authors":"Ofir Schlisselberg, Tal Lancewicki, Peter Auer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Modern delayed-bandit bounds matching lower bounds and emphasizing cumulative delay/missing observations, relevant to adaptive truncation alternatives.","title":"Improved Best-of-Both-Worlds Regret for Bandits with Delayed Feedback","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.24193","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2025 / arXiv:2505.24193"},{"authors":"Saeed Masoudian, Julian Zimmert, Yevgeny Seldin","relevance":"Robust delayed-bandit algorithm using skipping and outstanding-observation counts; conceptually useful for replacing hard tail cutoffs by missing-information penalties.","title":"A Best-of-both-worlds Algorithm for Bandits with Delayed Feedback with Robustness to Excessive Delays","url":"https://openreview.net/forum?id=LDzrQB4X5w","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2024"},{"authors":"Haipeng Luo, Robert E. Schapire","relevance":"Directly relevant background for unknown/adversarial horizon quantifiers and horizon-aware versus horizon-oblivious online learning.","title":"Towards Minimax Online Learning with Unknown Time Horizon","url":"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v32/luo14.html","venue_year":"ICML 2014, PMLR 32(1):226-234"},{"authors":"Eyal Gofer, Yishay Mansour","relevance":"Studies individual-sequence anytime regret over prefixes; useful for positioning the same-path prefix-conflict lower bound.","title":"Lower bounds on individual sequence regret","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10994-015-5531-y","venue_year":"Machine Learning 103(1):1-26, 2016"},{"authors":"Cristobal Guzman, Nishant Mehta, Ali Mortazavi","relevance":"Clarifies best-case and anytime-prefix notions in online learning; useful for avoiding quantifier confusion in prefix lower bounds.","title":"Best-case lower bounds in online learning","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2021/hash/b7da6669894867f04b8727876a69ffc0-Abstract.html","venue_year":"NeurIPS 2021"},{"authors":"Shie Mannor, John N. Tsitsiklis, Jia Yuan Yu","relevance":"Sample-path constraint framework may help formulate simultaneous-prefix compatibility or approachability-style feasibility conditions.","title":"Online Learning with Sample Path Constraints","url":"https://jmlr.org/beta/papers/v10/mannor09a.html","venue_year":"JMLR 10:569-590, 2009"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"Quantifier mistakes are the main risk: process-unaware, horizon-aware, randomized, and adaptive-adversary variants differ. Cross-block pending rewards and strict-threshold suprema also need careful epsilon accounting.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","quantifier audit","counterexample search"],"summary":"Turn the solver's concatenated universal block sketch into a theorem. For any randomized learner that does not know future continuations, choose dominant blocks 0^{N_i} c_i with c_i in {1/2,1}, M_i=o(N_i), and prove limsup_T R_T/T >= 1/4. Check the coupling after a fixed prefix, the old-reward cap by M_i, the eta-comparator version for strict crossing, and beta/gamma failure on the constructed sequence.","title":"Formalize Universal Block Lower Bound","why_promising":"The finite two-continuation lemma and prefix-cap fragment are already accepted; this is the shortest path from solver sketch to a real class-uniform impossibility theorem."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The policy model must be exact: a process-aware policy may know the entire infinite path but not the evaluation horizon. Need ensure later blocks cannot retroactively affect regret at the paired endpoints.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","model definition","edge-case audit"],"summary":"Formalize the same-path horizon-oblivious theorem for the deterministic path made of blocks 0^{N_i}, 1/2, 1. Prove that for endpoints T_i^0=M_i+N_i+1 and T_i^1=M_i+N_i+2, any horizon-oblivious policy satisfies max{R_{T_i^0},R_{T_i^1}} >= N_i(1/4-o(1))-M_i-O(1).","title":"Known-Path Horizon-Oblivious Obstruction","why_promising":"It cleanly separates the horizon-aware oracle collapse from anytime learning, using one fixed deterministic process rather than a process class."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"A family pi_T may be allowed to change its current-block behavior at each endpoint, so the infinite construction may require a diagonal or endpoint-specific adversarial choice. The result may only hold for oblivious adversaries or for fixed algorithms.","suggested_lenses":["quantifier audit","proof strategy"],"summary":"Investigate whether the universal block lower bound extends to horizon-aware learner families pi_T that know T but not the continuation sequence. The finite two-continuation gadget has the same horizon under c=1/2 and c=1, suggesting an endpoint-wise construction may work; write or refute the diagonalization carefully.","title":"Horizon-Aware Universal Learners","why_promising":"Many regret statements allow the horizon as input. Settling this would make the lower-bound regime much clearer than the current anytime-only statement."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"The quantity may become tautological, equivalent to minimax regret itself, or too hard to estimate. A useful definition must explain CE-001 as easy despite beta failure.","suggested_lenses":["definition design","lower-bound strategy","counterexample search"],"summary":"Define a decision-relevant prefix-incompatibility modulus: over pairs of continuations or same-path horizon pairs sharing a public prefix, take the smallest possible worst regret of any common-prefix action rule. Test whether finite blocks give modulus about N/4 while sparse vanishing spikes have small modulus despite beta(h)=1.","title":"Prefix-Incompatibility Tail Substitute","why_promising":"Current evidence says delay length is not the obstruction; incompatible optimal prefixes before resolving information are. A modulus of this kind could become the right necessity statement."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Positive-payoff rare branches may still create nonnegligible comparator value unless probabilities decay fast enough. Essential-sup tails require positive-probability branches at all large delays, and all-horizon regret bounds are easy to get wrong.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample construction","horizon accounting"],"summary":"Build a complete rare-history process where beta or gamma fails by essential supremum on tiny positive-probability branches, while expected pseudo-regret is o(T) for a simple process-aware or universal learner. Compute both conditional tails and comparator values for all horizons.","title":"Rare-History Essential-Sup Stress Test","why_promising":"It would show that even positive-scale conditional tail conditions can be too strong under expected pseudo-regret, strengthening the case for averaged or information-based quantities."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"Most cited algorithms are bandit/OCO rather than public-price full-information threshold rewards. The market-making reduction must specify exactly when each grid arm's reward is known and how skipped fills affect comparator regret.","suggested_lenses":["literature mapping","algorithm design","proof strategy"],"summary":"Translate adaptive skipping and outstanding-observation delayed-learning results to the grid market-making problem. Aim for a sufficient theorem that does not require a known H and is bounded by skipped payoff mass or value-weighted outstanding rewards, not by sup_h beta(h).","title":"Adaptive Skipping Sufficiency","why_promising":"Recent delayed-feedback literature increasingly treats missing information, not elapsed time, as the complexity measure; this matches the counterexamples where long delays are predictable or irrelevant."},{"id":"idea_07","risks":"The delayed setting may still require many active breakpoints, and improved discretization will not address necessity. Strict crossing and atoms mean exact maximizers may still fail without left-limit conventions.","suggested_lenses":["structural analysis","algorithm design"],"summary":"Exploit the identity r_t^H(a)=a 1{M_t^H>a}, where M_t^H is the future-window maximum price. Study whether the continuum comparator can be handled exactly by intervals between observed prices, left limits, or adaptive grids, reducing or removing the T/K discretization term in the sufficiency theorem.","title":"Threshold Geometry For Continuum Quotes","why_promising":"It could turn the sufficient theorem into a cleaner continuum result and clarify sup-versus-max pathologies without relying on a crude uniform grid."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
