logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2452:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2458:- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2470:- Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, *Prediction, Learning, and Games* (Cambridge University Press, 2006), verified from the authors' official book page. It is useful for standard online-learning quantifiers: algorithms working for all individual sequences relative to a reference class, expert advice, and minimax regret.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2476:- Gofer and Mansour (Machine Learning 2016) is verified. It lower-bounds individual-sequence anytime regret for broad online linear optimization forecasters. Relevant background for anytime-prefix lower bounds, but the market-making prefix conflict is a separate direct argument.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2492:- Bernstein and Shimkin (JMLR 2015) is verified. Response-based approachability may be algorithmically relevant if finite-prefix dual constraints are recast as vector-deficit feasibility; no direct delayed-fill result.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2501:- Wu, Grama, and Szpankowski (COLT/PMLR 2023), "Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments," is verified. Its process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are parameterized by switching or drought complexity; not direct proof support.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2507:- Fulkerson and Gross (Pacific Journal of Mathematics 1965), "Incidence matrices and interval graphs," is verified. It is relevant background for consecutive-ones/interval-incidence structure in the finite-alphabet dual, but it does not imply the desired uncrossing/localization theorem because the market dual has nested maxima.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2509:- Lindley (Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 1952), "The theory of queues with a single server," is verified. It is useful only as background for possible reflected-surplus/debt recursions in two-level drought bookkeeping; it is not a market-making result.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2510:- Ayer, Brunk, Ewing, Reid, and Silverman (Annals of Mathematical Statistics 1955) is verified as the classical source behind pooling adjacent violators/isotonic estimation. It is relevant proof-technology background for possible pooling rules in unequal-low drought LPs; it does not prove the reduced drought LP or a delayed-market-making theorem.
logs/iter_018_solver.stderr.log:2511:- Chakravarti (Mathematics of Operations Research 1989) is verified. It treats isotonic median regression via linear programming and is useful only as an analogy for deriving pooling/PAVA-like algorithms from LP duals.
