logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2738:- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2748:- Bistritz, Zhou, Chen, Bambos, and Blanchet (JMLR 2022), "No Weighted-Regret Learning in Adversarial Bandits with Delays," is verified. It studies delayed adversarial bandits with weighted-regret objectives and bounds involving total delay; relevant background for horizon-weighted finite-prefix dual certificates, not a delayed-fill characterization theorem.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2773:- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2779:- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2791:- Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, *Prediction, Learning, and Games* (Cambridge University Press, 2006), verified from the authors' official book page. It is useful for standard online-learning quantifiers: algorithms working for all individual sequences relative to a reference class, expert advice, and minimax regret.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2797:- Gofer and Mansour (Machine Learning 2016) is verified. It lower-bounds individual-sequence anytime regret for broad online linear optimization forecasters. Relevant background for anytime-prefix lower bounds, but the market-making prefix conflict is a separate direct argument.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2813:- Bernstein and Shimkin (JMLR 2015) is verified. Response-based approachability may be algorithmically relevant if finite-prefix dual constraints are recast as vector-deficit feasibility; no direct delayed-fill result.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2822:- Wu, Grama, and Szpankowski (COLT/PMLR 2023), "Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments," is verified. Its process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are parameterized by switching or drought complexity; not direct proof support.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2828:- Fulkerson and Gross (Pacific Journal of Mathematics 1965), "Incidence matrices and interval graphs," is verified. It is relevant background for consecutive-ones/interval-incidence structure in the finite-alphabet dual, but it does not imply the desired uncrossing/localization theorem because the market dual has nested maxima.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2830:- Lindley (Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 1952), "The theory of queues with a single server," is verified. It is useful only as background for possible reflected-surplus/debt recursions in two-level drought bookkeeping; it is not a market-making result.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2831:- Ayer, Brunk, Ewing, Reid, and Silverman (Annals of Mathematical Statistics 1955) is verified as the classical source behind pooling adjacent violators/isotonic estimation. It is relevant proof-technology background for possible pooling rules in unequal-low drought LPs; it does not prove the reduced drought LP or a delayed-market-making theorem.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2832:- Chakravarti (Mathematics of Operations Research 1989) is verified. It treats isotonic median regression via linear programming and is useful only as an analogy for deriving pooling/PAVA-like algorithms from LP duals.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2833:- Federgruen and Groenevelt (Operations Research 1986) is verified. It studies greedy optimality for resource-allocation problems with nested/group constraints; relevant as a possible proof-technology analogy for front-loaded low-mass allocation, not direct support for the drought formula.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2855:Iteration 7 sharpens the internal threshold geometry: the exact \(m\)-positive-support local gap is \(((m-1)/m)^m\), approaching \(1/e\). The new price-discrimination/robust-pricing sources above are useful context for equal-revenue and minimax duality, but the finite-support constant and the terminal-block \(1/e\) universal lift should currently be cited as internal results, not as imported theorems.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2865:Iteration 12 adds internal exact finite-prefix machinery for deterministic known exogenous paths: the marginal reduction, finite-prefix LP dual \(D_n(P)\), and fixed-envelope compactness criterion. Literature checks found no direct theorem for this delayed-fill characterization. Lagziel-Lehrer 2015 is relevant delayed-approachability background for future vector-deficit formulations; Bistritz et al. 2022 is relevant weighted-regret delayed-bandit background for horizon-weighted certificates; Xie-Zhu-Shishkin 2025 is adjacent pricing context. All three should be cited as background only.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2867:Iteration 13 found no external theorem proving \(D_n=o(n)\Rightarrow o(T)\), nor a known market-path separator from the fixed-envelope criterion. The added approachability and uniform-value sources are background for possible formulations and for gluing cautions. Current internal proof work should focus on the last-exceedance/finite-prefix dual structure rather than importing uniform-value theorems wholesale.
logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:2869:Iteration 14 found no direct external theorem proving gluing or a separator. The internal progress is an exact separated-block value, an exact dual boundary identity, and a finite-alphabet dual reduction. Fulkerson-Gross supports the interval-incidence vocabulary for the finite-alphabet formula, Renault 2014 and existing uniform-value sources reinforce the need for stability beyond scalar finite-prefix values, and pseudonorm/approachability and countably-infinite LP sources are possible formulation tools only.
